        Takes a list of githooks to disable as parameters. Must be called from the git_toplevel.
        Fails when a hook is not pointing to bar.

  help - Provides help, extracts documentation from modules
    
    Inline documentation uses two or three hash characters. Three '###' is used for file level
    documentation to describe a file entirely. Two '##' are used in different contexts:
    
    Variable definitions that use the 'declare' keyword can use '##' at the end of the same
    line to document the variable.
    
    Functions use formal parameter syntax: 'function name ## [--opt] <required> [optional] - description'
    Parameters use: <mandatory>, [optional], param.., [param..], --flags, foo|bar alternatives.
    Multi-line documentation continues on following lines starting with optional whitespace
    followed by '##' and parameter explanations.
    
    Rules are documented by '##' lines before the rule definition, using the same formal
    parameter syntax when the rule accepts parameters.
    
    Only variables, functions and rules that have doc comments are included in the output.
    
    Documentation says what a thing does and how to use it.  The reasoning behind it -- why it is
    written that way, what was measured -- belongs in single '#' source comments, which are not
    published.

    Rules:

      help [[-s|--short] topic|rule|function|variable|text]
        Show help topics
        - [-s|--short]:                 Show only the queried sections
        - topic|rule|function|variable: Navigate to or show only specified topics
        - text:                         Search the specified text in the documentation

  helpers_rules - The helper repertoire: deterministic answers to recurring retrieval questions.
    
    A *helper* is a bar rule which answers one recurring, semi deterministic question -- a filter,
    a retrieval, a listing, a count, a state snapshot -- in a single invocation, with a bounded
    and stable output.  Its consumer is an agent: with a helper the question costs one command and
    a few lines of context, without it several turns of ad hoc pipelines.  Helpers filter, count
    and quote, they never summarise and never judge.  Where a question is naturally a *check*
    (pass or fail), it is written as a check target and reported by 'bar check'
    (Bar.d/agent_rules) instead of being squeezed into a helper.
    
      bar helpers              the repertoire: one line per helper, then the total count
      bar helpers --json       the same repertoire as one JSON object (same count)
      bar helpers --check      verify that every helper module is registered (see below)
      please helpers           as 'bar', plus the repertoire of the current directory
    
    The repertoire is read out of the module sources: nothing is loaded and no helper is executed
    to list it, and 'please helpers' works from any directory.  Which helpers are listed depends
    on the *personality* bar was called by -- 'bar' or 'please' (see REGISTRATION below).
    
    HELPER MODULES AND RULE NAMES
    
    The helpers of one domain live together in one *helper module*, 'Bar.d/<word>', which names
    itself in its file header:
    
      # helper-module: <word>
    
    Every rule of a helper module is named '<word>' or '<word>_<rest>', because bar derives the
    module to autoload from the word before the first underscore of a rule name ('always_',
    'is_', 'has_' and 'try_' are stripped first, see 'rule_autoload' in Bar.d/std_lib).  A helper
    rule therefore loads exactly its own module: invoking one helper never loads another helper
    module, and listing the repertoire loads none at all.  '<word>' must be a word no other module
    claims -- bar ships 'git', 'rad', 'cargo', 'net', 'ssh', 'testdir', ... -- because a
    rule named 'rad_state' would autoload 'Bar.d/rad' and then fail to exist.  Pick a free word.
    
    Everything in a helper module that is not an entry point is a *function*: functions are not
    rules, the autoloader never looks for them and the repertoire never lists them.
    
    REGISTRATION
    
    A helper registers itself with one declaration line at column 0, directly above its rule
    definition (only comments and blank lines in between, in the same file):
    
      # helper:        <name> | <purpose> | <invocation> | <output shape>
      # helper-bar:    <name> | <purpose> | <invocation> | <output shape>
      # helper-please: <name> | <purpose> | <invocation> | <output shape>
    
      <name>        the rule name: '<word>' or '<word>_<rest>' of that module
      <purpose>     one line, the question it answers -- this is how an agent picks a helper
      <invocation>  how to call it, its options included ('hermeskanban [--json] <selector>')
      <output>      one line, the shape of the answer
    
    The three forms share the four fields and the adjacency rule and differ only in *which
    personality* lists the helper.  A plain '# helper:' is generic and is listed by both; a
    '# helper-bar:' is listed only when bar was called as 'bar', a '# helper-please:' only when
    it was called as 'please'.  Every listed row carries the personality: the invocation field is
    prefixed with it, so 'bar helpers' prints 'bar hermeskanban [--json]' and 'please helpers'
    prints the same 'please hermeskanban [--json]', and each JSON object carries that name in a
    'command' field.  The kind never weakens the guard: an adjacency, naming, unusable-field or
    duplicate violation is reported for every declaration no matter which personality it addresses.
    
    'please helpers' also runs 'bar helpers' in the current working directory and appends those
    rows, each labelled with the 'bar' personality -- the vendored or project repertoire of the
    place it is run in.  There is no dedup: a generic '# helper:' is then listed twice, once as
    the site's 'please' helper and once as the directory's 'bar' helper, and the two may even run
    different code.  The count line still counts every listed row.  The child is invoked directly,
    not resolved through '$PATH', and can not recurse (a child is called as 'bar'); when it lists
    nothing -- no 'Bar.d' here and none installed -- the union is simply empty.  '--check' is not
    affected: it validates the personality's own module directory (the site directory for 'please').
    
    Neither field may contain a '|' and a declaration is one line.  There is no index file: the
    repertoire is *generated* by scanning the modules, so a new helper is one rule plus one
    declaration and nothing else has to be kept in sync.  'bar helpers --check' (hooked into
    'lints' of bar's own Barf) fails when a rule of a helper module is not declared, when a
    declaration does not resolve to the rule below it, when a helper is declared twice, when its
    name would autoload another module, when the module header names another file, when a
    declaration sits in a file that is no helper module, when a declaration is separated from its
    rule by other code or when it uses an unknown kind ('# helper-foo:' where one of the three
    forms above was expected).
    
    The index lists exactly the declarations the guard accepts and rejects nothing the index
    shows: both are views of the same single validation pass, so a helper which is not listed is
    always a reported violation and a listed helper always resolves.  A rejected declaration is
    counted on stderr and named by 'bar helpers --check'.
    
    OUTPUT CONVENTIONS (requirements, not style)
    
      * print what the consumer needs for the next step: output << input;
      * the shape is the helper's choice (lines, table, JSON) but stable and documented, counts
        are an explicit line of their own and never implied;
      * print less than everything only together with the exact elision counts ('n of N') and the
        path of a file holding the complete, untruncated data, so nothing is lost;
      * exit codes: 0 answered, non-zero could not answer, and a distinct code where the caller
        has to tell 'no data' from 'error'; an empty result is an answer, not a failure;
      * stdout carries only the result -- no progress chatter and no ANSI sequences -- stderr may
        carry diagnostics, and a helper never pages interactively ('less' blocks an agent turn,
        see Bar.d/search for that anti-pattern);
      * read the data directly (file, CLI, sqlite, git) instead of going through an interface
        which refuses to run outside its own context;
      * '--json' stays opt-in: text for reading, one object for a wrapper.
    
    A helper module needs no edit anywhere else: it becomes reachable for 'please' with
    'bar init_install <prefix>' (which links every module that is not linked yet into
    '~/.config/please', see Bar.d/init) and for a project with 'bar init --update' (which copies
    new modules into the project's 'Bar.d/').

    Functions:

      helpers_module_of <rule>
        the module 'rule_autoload' loads for <rule>
        Mirrors 'rule_autoload' in Bar.d/std_lib: the prefixes are stripped, then everything from
        the first underscore on.  'rad_state' -> 'rad', 'has_hermes_logs' -> 'hermes'.

      helpers_personality - 'bar' or 'please', the personality this invocation lists for
        Bar has two personalities (see the module header and Bar.d/init): 'bar' is the project and
        installation one, 'please' the private one of the single site directory '~/.config/please'.
        The name bar was called by is in 'BAR_CALLED_AS' (set in the bar script from '$0'), anything
        but 'please' is treated as 'bar' so a direct 'bar', './bar' or an odd symlink still lists
        the repertoire a project expects.

      helpers_union_child [args..]
        'bar helpers' in the cwd for the 'please' union
        'please helpers' lists the repertoire of the place it is run in as well: 'bar helpers' in
        the current working directory, which is the vendored or project repertoire there.  The child
        is invoked directly through '$BAR_SELF' and never through a '$PATH' lookup, so a 'please'
        can not pick a foreign 'bar'; it is called as 'bar', so it can not recurse into a union of
        its own.  A 'bar' which can not list a repertoire here -- no 'Bar.d' in sight and none
        installed -- contributes nothing and its diagnostics are swallowed: an empty half is not an
        error of 'please helpers'.

      helpers_union_rows - the 'bar' rows the 'please' union appends, without their count

      helpers_records - scan '$BAR_DIR' for the repertoire, as US (0x1f) separated records
        One awk pass over the module files of '$BAR_DIR', nothing is loaded and no helper is run.
        The records feed the single validation pass below:
          <file> <line> M <word>                                       a '# helper-module:' header
          <file> <line> D <name> <purpose> <usage> <output> <n> <word> <kind> a declaration
          <file> <line> U <kind>                                       an unknown '# helper-<kind>:' line
          <file> <line> R <rule>                                       a rule of a helper module
          <file> <line> S                                              the code line separating a
                                                                       declaration from its rule
        'D' and 'U' records are emitted for every file -- a declaration outside a helper module is a
        violation and has to be seen, and so is an unknown kind -- while 'R' and 'S' are only emitted
        for a file which names a helper module, so a module without helpers costs nothing.  'S' is
        the one code line that separates a declaration from its rule; it is decided here and not by
        the walk, so the walk costs one iteration per declaration and rule instead of one per line of
        a helper module.  The kind of a 'D' record is 'generic' for a plain '# helper:', 'bar' for
        '# helper-bar:' and 'please' for '# helper-please:' and decides which personality lists it.
        A file which neither names a helper module nor declares a helper contributes no record at all
        and is dropped before the walk, with one 'grep -l' that stops at the first matching line: the
        index stays cheap while the module sources grow (a byte of a module nobody declares a helper
        in costs a 'grep' byte, not an 'awk' line), and the records are the ones the walk would have
        produced from the whole directory.
        Fields are separated by US (0x1f) and not by a tab: a declaration may have empty fields
        (that is one of the violations) and a tab is IFS whitespace, which 'read' would collapse.

      helpers_declaration_error <name> <purpose> <usage> <output> <fields> <word>
        why is a declaration unusable?
        Prints the reason, and nothing when the declaration is usable.  One implementation for the
        whole module.

      helpers_walk - the one validation pass behind the index and the guard
        Walks the records of 'helpers_records' and prints, in file and line order:
          !<file>:<line>: <problem>                        every violation
          +<name> <purpose> <usage> <output> <word> <file>  a declaration which resolves
        A declaration is only accepted once the rule directly below it has been seen, so this
        single pass covers registration, resolution, adjacency and naming at once.  The index shows
        the '+<...>' lines and the guard reports the '!<...>' lines, which is what keeps the two
        from drifting apart.  A declaration is listed only when its kind addresses the personality
        of this invocation ('generic' always, 'bar' only for 'bar', 'please' only for 'please'),
        while every violation is reported no matter which kind it belongs to.
        leaving a file: an open declaration and a module without helpers are violations
        there.  The sentinel record below leaves the last file through this same branch.

      helpers_declarations - the registered helpers as '<name> <purpose> <usage> <output> <word> <file>'
        Sorted by name; the file is the module file as it was found in '$BAR_DIR'.  The violations
        'helpers_walk' reports are counted on stderr, the guard names each one.

      helpers_index_text - the text index: one row per helper, then the count line
        The rows of this personality, then ('please' only) the rows of the union, then a split line
        and the count line.  The invocation of every row is prefixed with the personality it belongs
        to, and the count is the number of listed rows -- the same total the JSON form reports.

      helpers_index_json - the JSON index: one object per helper, count and both halves of a union
        One object: 'bar_helpers':1, the count and one object per listed helper.  Every object
        carries the personality in 'command' and has it prefixed on 'invocation' (a wrapper can tell
        a 'please' helper from a 'bar' one).  For 'please' the 'bar' repertoire of the current
        directory is merged in as well -- summed count, both halves' objects, again without dedup.

      helpers_check_body - the registration guard for every helper module
        Prints every violation 'helpers_walk' reports, one line each, and returns 1; prints nothing
        and returns 0 when the repertoire is complete.

      helpers [--json|--check]
        the repertoire, see the module header
        Body of the 'helpers' rule: the index (text or JSON) or the registration guard.  The
        machine form is also entered with 'bar --json helpers' (BAR_AGENT_JSON, see
        Bar.d/agent_rules).  An unknown argument is a usage error, exit 2.

    Rules:

      helpers
        The repertoire index: one line per registered helper ('<name>  <purpose>  | <invocation>  |
        <output shape>') sorted by name, then the total count as the last line.  '--json' prints one
        object with the same count, '--check' verifies the registration of every helper module.

      helpers_check
        Verify that every helper module registers its rules ('bar helpers --check'): the guard is a
        check target, so 'bar check helpers_check' reports it with the agent contract (verdict, chain,
        totals, log path) and 'rule lints: helpers_check' can hook it into the lint target.

  hermes - Retrieval over the Hermes log corpus -- what the logs of this host say about <X>, in one call
    
    Hermes writes a corpus of text on this host: the agent and gateway logs, one worker transcript
    per kanban task, one output document per cron run, and the session store.  The corpus is large
    (megabytes, millions of lines) and reading it with ad hoc pipelines -- 'grep' here, 'sed' there,
    a 'sqlite3' query next -- costs several turns and floods an agent's context with raw
    transcripts.  'hermes_logs' is the one deterministic answer instead: it streams the corpus once,
    prints a bounded digest of the matching lines and keeps the complete match list on disk.
    
      please hermes_logs --grep <ere> --since 1d      # a bounded digest and the exact total
      please hermes_logs --grep <ere> --count         # only the number
      please hermes_logs --grep <ere> --json | jq .   # one object for a wrapper
    
    THE CORPUS (explicit, never guessed)
    
    One *corpus root* is a Hermes home directory; the default is '$HERMES_HOME' and falls back to
    '~/.hermes'.  A root contributes the sources below, and only these -- nothing is searched
    implicitly, no directory is discovered by walking:
    
      logs       <root>/logs/*.log, *.log.gz             the agent/gateway/gui logs
      workers    <root>/kanban/logs/*.log, *.log.gz      one worker transcript per kanban task
      cron       any file below <root>/cron/output/      'cron/output/<job>/<stamp>.md' and the
                                                          flat legacy 'cron/output/<job>_*.txt'
      sessions   <root>/sessions/*.jsonl, *.jsonl.gz     diverted (transcript) session records
      store      <root>/state.db                         the SQLite session store, read only
    
    The root is overridable with '--root <dir>' (repeatable) or with the environment variable
    BAR_HERMES_LOGS_ROOT (':' separated); '--root' wins.  Other Hermes profiles are addressed with
    '--root ~/.hermes/profiles/<name>', never by guessing: a path that differs between the gateway,
    the TUI and a worker is exactly what an agent must not hardcode.  With more than one root every
    locator carries the index of its root ('#2/logs/agent.log:88') and one 'corpus:' line names each.
    
    THE ANSWER (one record per matching line)
    
      <locator> <TAB> <timestamp> <TAB> <snippet>
    
      locator       '<source path>:<line>' relative to its root ('logs/agent.log:1234'), so
                    'sed -n 1234p <root>/logs/agent.log' retrieves the line byte for byte.  A store
                    record has no file line and reads 'state.db:<session>/<message>/<line>', which
                    'sqlite3 -readonly <root>/state.db' resolves with 'where id=<message>'.
      timestamp     'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' local time -- the stamp on the line when it has one (at the
                    line start for a log line, anywhere on a JSON record), otherwise the stamp of the
                    newest line above it that had one, otherwise the file's modification time.  A
                    leading '~' marks those two derived cases, an exact stamp carries no marker.  A
                    store record carries the message's own timestamp.
      snippet       the line with its control characters removed, clipped at
                    BAR_HERMES_LOGS_SNIPPET characters (default 160) with the number of characters
                    left out appended as '<+N>'.  Tabs separate the fields; a snippet never holds one.
    
    Then the totals, as lines of their own and never implied:
    
      corpus: <root> [<sources scanned>]   matches: <n>   shown: <s> of <n> (limit <l> lines, <c> chars)
      clipped: <k> snippet(s) at <c> chars   unreadable: <u> source(s) not scanned: <name>..
      max: <path>
    
    'matches' is the exact number of matching lines, a hard assertion: it is the count the scan
    produced over exactly the sources the 'corpus:' lines name, and it is qualified by 'unreadable'
    when a source could not be read.  The digest prints at most '--limit' match lines (default 20,
    BAR_HERMES_LOGS_LIMIT) and at most BAR_HERMES_LOGS_BYTES characters of them (default 1200), so
    the answer stays around a kilobyte however busy the corpus is.  Whenever anything stays unshown
    the complete match list is on disk before anything is printed and its absolute path is printed as
    'max:'.  That file holds every match ('<locator> <TAB> <timestamp> <TAB> the whole line'), up to
    BAR_HERMES_LOGS_MAX records (default 20000) -- beyond that the file is capped and 'max:' says so.
    It is written below BAR_HERMES_LOGS_DIR (default $TMPDIR) and named after the query, so the same
    question reuses one file and the answer stays retrievable.
    
    FILTERS
    
      --grep <ere>     an extended regular expression, matched against every line of every scanned
                       source with 'grep -E' semantics.  Without it every line matches (the digest is
                       still bounded, the count still exact).  It is the only thing that decides what
                       counts: no keyword prefilter, no sampling, no ranking.
      --since <when>   '<n>[smhdw]' (ago) or anything GNU 'date -d' accepts ('2026-10-10',
                       '2026-10-10 08:00:00'); records before the cutoff are dropped.  In the store
                       the range is pushed into SQL, which makes a bounded session query cheap.
      --source <kind>  restrict to a source kind, repeatable.
      --task <sel>     keep only the sources whose path contains <sel>: '--task t_deadbeef' keeps a
                       worker transcript, '--run 2026-10-10' the cron run of that day.  Applied to
                       the source path, so the store never matches a task selector.
      --session <id>   keep only the records of that session: store rows whose session id contains it
                       (in SQL), 'sessions/<id>.jsonl' files by name, and the lines of every other
                       source by their text (the agent logs carry '[<session-id>]').
      --count          the number alone -- it wins over '--json', because one number is already the
                       machine form.  Nothing is elided here, so no 'max:' line is printed.
    
    EXIT CODES: 0 answered, matches found.  3 answered, no match ('grep' semantics: an answer, not a
    failure -- the count is printed).  2 usage error.  1 could not answer: a root is not a directory,
    or a source could not be read -- the digest is still printed, marked with 'unreadable:' and with
    the reason on stderr, and the count then covers only what was readable.
    
    NON GOALS: no summarisation, no judgement, no LLM, no ranking -- it is a filter.  It does not
    replace the 'session_search' tool for fuzzy recall, and it does not read what Hermes stores
    outside the paths above (the kanban board as a database, cron job definitions, the vault).  It
    never pages, never colours, and prints no progress on stdout.
    
    HANDOFF BRIEFS ('hermes_handoff')
    
    A child session ('delegate_task') starts with no memory of the conversation that spawned it, so
    the caller hands it a brief.  'hermes_handoff' composes that brief, resolves its anchor phrases
    against the session store and checks that a child could retrieve the session -- one rule, three
    modes:
    
      hermes_handoff --goal <line> [--context <text> | --context-file <path>]
                     [--anchor <phrase>].. [--gate <line>].. [--report-chars <n>]
                     [--session <id>] [--db <path>] [--json]
          a brief in seven sections, in order, one heading each, plain text: GOAL, CONTEXT,
          RETRIEVAL, ANCHORS, MECHANICS, ACCEPTANCE GATES and REPORT FORMAT.  GOAL, CONTEXT and
          every --gate line are printed verbatim; each --anchor phrase resolves to its messages.
      hermes_handoff --anchors <phrase>.. [--limit <n>] [--session <id>] [--db <path>] [--json]
          one 'msg <id> <role> <snippet>' line per distinct matching message, then 'matches: <n>'.
      hermes_handoff --verify [--session <id>] [--db <path>] [--json]
          whether a child could retrieve this session: 'session <id> msgs=<n> chars=<n>
          retrieval=ok|missing', then the two retrieval recipes and the live delegation caps.
    
    THE SESSION STORE
    
    The store is a read only SQLite database, '${HERMES_HOME:-$HOME/.hermes}/state.db', overridden by
    '--db <path>' or '$HANDOFF_DB'.  The session id comes from '--session <id>' or
    '$HERMES_SESSION_ID'; the profile -- the first segment of an '@session:<profile>/<id>' link --
    from '$HERMES_SESSION_PROFILE', default 'default'.  'messages' and 'sessions' are read with the
    'sqlite3' CLI: no LLM and no summariser.  An anchor phrase resolves to the messages whose
    'content' contains it, case insensitively; a phrase that resolves to nothing is reported as
    'UNRESOLVED: <phrase>' and never guessed.  Nothing host local is hardcoded: the caps are read
    live with 'hermes config get delegation.<key>' and '$HERMES_MAX_ITERATIONS'.
    
    EXIT CODES
    
      0 answered; 1 --anchors matched nothing; 2 usage error; 3 the session or the store is unknown
      or unreadable; 4 the brief has an unresolved anchor (the whole brief is still printed).
    
    The text and the '--json' forms carry the same numbers; '--json' is one object.
    

    Variables:

      BAR_HERMES_LOGS_ROOT (gx)
        ':' separated corpus roots, '--root' wins
        Default: ${BAR_HERMES_LOGS_ROOT:-}

      BAR_HERMES_LOGS_LIMIT (gx)
        match lines the digest prints
        Default: ${BAR_HERMES_LOGS_LIMIT:-20}

      BAR_HERMES_LOGS_SNIPPET (gx)
        characters kept of a matching line
        Default: ${BAR_HERMES_LOGS_SNIPPET:-160}

      BAR_HERMES_LOGS_MAX (gx)
        records kept in the full match list
        Default: ${BAR_HERMES_LOGS_MAX:-20000}

      BAR_HERMES_LOGS_BYTES (gx)
        characters of match records the digest prints
        Default: ${BAR_HERMES_LOGS_BYTES:-1200}

      BAR_HERMES_LOGS_DIR (gx)
        where the full match list is written
        Default: ${BAR_HERMES_LOGS_DIR:-}

    Functions:

      hermes_logs_selector <option> <value>
        reject a selector neither SQL nor the shell can carry

      hermes_logs_since <when>
        resolve '--since' into HERMES_LOGS_Q_CUTOFF and Q_EPOCH
        The cutoff is the local 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' the line timestamps are compared against (for
        that shape a lexicographic comparison is a chronological one), the epoch is the same instant
        for the SQL range.  Both are set here, or the call fails.

      hermes_logs_wanted <kind> [kind..]
        is this source kind wanted? (no kind: all)

      hermes_logs_emit_files <root> <index> <kind> [file..]
        source records, sorted by path
        One record per file: '<kind>\t<index>\t<relpath>\t<mtime>', the mtime in the shape of a line
        timestamp, so a source without timestamps is still dated.

      hermes_logs_files <root> <index> [kind..]
        the corpus of one root as source records

      hermes_logs_program - the awk filter, one program for a text source and for the store
        It reads its query from the environment ('HL_*') and not from 'awk -v': '-v' would interpret
        backslash escapes in the pattern and silently search for a different expression.  It streams:
        one line in, at most two records out (the full match list and the digest), never a buffer of
        matches.  A store row is '<session> 0x1f <message> 0x1f <timestamp> 0x1f <content>', the
        content's newlines encoded as 0x01.

      hermes_logs_scan_file <file> <locator> <mtime> <session-filter>
        scan one text source

      hermes_logs_scan_store <db> <locator> <mtime>
        scan the session store, read only
        The store is read with 'sqlite3 -readonly': it never creates a journal and never touches the
        live writer's WAL.  '--since' is pushed into SQL and the session selector becomes
        'instr(session_id, ..)' so that it stays a substring match and no LIKE wildcard changes its
        meaning.  The content is deliberately *not* prefetched by keyword: the pattern decides what
        counts, in awk, exactly as it does for a file.  A newline inside a message is encoded as 0x01
        and a stray 0x1f as 0x1e, so one row is one input line; both are control characters and are
        removed from the snippet anyway.

      hermes_logs_used <kind> <root-index>
        remember which sources one root contributed
        The digest: the bounded match lines, then the totals as lines of their own.
        The same numbers and the same records as one object, for a wrapper.

      hermes_logs_scan - scan the corpus of HERMES_LOGS_Q_ROOTS and print the answer
        Streams every source once, appends every match to the full match list (bounded by
        HERMES_LOGS_Q_MAX), keeps the digest records (bounded by HERMES_LOGS_Q_LIMIT) and prints one
        of the three answers.  Returns the exit code of the module's contract.
        Body of the 'hermes_logs' rule: parse the options into the query context, resolve the corpus
        roots and hand over to 'hermes_logs_scan'.  See the module header for the contract.

      hermes_handoff_home - the Hermes home directory
        '$HERMES_HOME' when set, '~/.hermes' otherwise.  Resolved here and nowhere else so no path of
        one host leaks into the rule.

      hermes_handoff_db <override>
        the session store path
        '--db' wins, then '$HANDOFF_DB', then the store below the Hermes home.

      hermes_handoff_profile - the profile of an '@session:' link

      hermes_handoff_session <override>
        the session id, or empty

      hermes_handoff_trim <text>
        <text> without leading or trailing whitespace

      hermes_handoff_cap_text <value>
        <value>, or 'unavailable' when it is empty

      hermes_handoff_sql <db> <sql>
        run one read only query, US separated fields
        'sqlite3 -readonly' never creates a journal and never writes to the store.

      hermes_handoff_quote <value>
        a SQL single quoted literal

      hermes_handoff_match <phrase>
        the SQL predicate matching one anchor phrase
        A case insensitive substring match over the message content.  'instr' -- not 'like' -- is
        used so no wildcard of the phrase has a meaning of its own.

      hermes_handoff_store_ok <db>
        0 when <db> is a readable session store

      hermes_handoff_session_exists <db> <session>
        0 when the session is in the store

      hermes_handoff_resolve <db> <session> <phrase> [<limit>]
        the matching messages

      hermes_handoff_count <db> <session> <phrase>
        the number of matching messages

      hermes_handoff_stats <db> <session>
        the message count and total content size
        Sets HERMES_HANDOFF_MSGS and HERMES_HANDOFF_CHARS.

      hermes_handoff_record <record>
        'msg <id> <role> <snippet>' from one US record

      hermes_handoff_config <key>
        one 'hermes config get delegation.<key>' value, or empty

      hermes_handoff_caps - read the live delegation caps into the HERMES_HANDOFF_CAP_* variables
        Read live and never hardcoded: the spawn depth and the summary budget come from the Hermes
        configuration, the child iteration cap from '$HERMES_MAX_ITERATIONS' when set and the
        configuration otherwise.

      hermes_handoff_caps_line - one 'caps:' line

      hermes_handoff_recipes <db> <session>
        the two exact retrieval recipes

      hermes_handoff_json_strings <text..>
        the texts as a JSON array of strings

      hermes_handoff_json_records <record..>
        the US records as a JSON array of objects

      hermes_handoff_usage - the usage text, on stderr

      hermes_handoff_brief_run <session> <db> <json> <goal> <context> <report_chars>

      hermes_handoff_anchors_json <session> <db> <limit> <matched> <printed> <full>

      hermes_handoff_anchors_run <session> <db> <json> <limit>
        Whatever the digest leaves out stays retrievable from a file whose path is printed next
        to the elision counts.  Written through a temporary name.

      hermes_handoff_verify_json <session> <db> <retrieval>

      hermes_handoff_verify_run <session> <db> <json>
        Body of the 'hermes_handoff' rule: parse the options, pick the mode and hand over.  See the
        'HANDOFF BRIEFS' section of the module header for the modes, the store and the exit codes.

    Rules:

      hermes_logs
        The one deterministic answer to 'what do the logs of this host say about <X>'.  Streams the
        corpus once (agent/gateway logs, kanban worker transcripts, cron run outputs, the session store),
        prints a bounded digest and the exact match count, and keeps the complete match list on disk.
        Exit 0 answered, 3 for no match, 2 usage error, 1 a source could not be read.  See the module
        header for the corpus, the locator format, the filters and the non goals.

      hermes_handoff
        One rule, three modes: a child session brief, the resolved anchor phrases, and whether a child
        could retrieve the session.  Reads the Hermes session store with 'sqlite3' and the delegation
        caps live -- no LLM.  Exit 0 answered, 1 --anchors matched nothing, 2 usage error, 3 the session
        or the store is unknown/unreadable, 4 the brief has an unresolved anchor.  See the module header.

  hermeskanban - The Hermes Kanban board state digest -- the board questions in one call
    
    Hermes Kanban board state: what the local board holds right now, bounded.
    
    'hermeskanban' answers the board questions that otherwise cost several turns of hand written
    sqlite -- and that 'hermes kanban' refuses to answer at all inside a worker context -- by
    reading the board database directly: the counts by status together with how stale the oldest
    ready card is and how many runs are live (the summary, which lists no card); the cards matching
    a filter, one per line, with their dependencies and their live run; why a blocked card is
    blocked; and what a card's runs did.
    
      hermeskanban                              the bounded summary digest, no card listed
      hermeskanban --status blocked             the blocked cards, one line each, with the reason
      hermeskanban --assignee default           the cards of one agent, highest priority first
      hermeskanban --parent t_deadbeef          the children of one card
      hermeskanban --runs t_deadbeef            the runs of one card, newest first
      hermeskanban --json                       the same numbers as one JSON object
    
    A listing is bounded by '--limit' (default 50) and prints the exact elision counts plus the path
    of a file holding every matching card whenever it elides anything; the summary always fits in
    six lines.  Nothing but the answer goes to stdout.
    
    EXIT CODES: 0 answered (a filter matching no card is an answer, not a failure), 2 usage error,
    3 the board could not be read (no database, not a Kanban database, 'sqlite3' missing), 4 the
    card asked for ('--id', '--parent', '--runs') does not exist.
    
    An option is nothing without its value: '--status' with nothing after it is a usage error like
    any other, and so is an empty value ('--id ""'), which would otherwise name no card and quietly
    answer as though the option had not been given at all.
    
    The board is resolved the way the kernel resolves it: '--db', then '--board', else
    '$HERMES_KANBAN_DB', '$HERMES_KANBAN_BOARD' or '<root>/kanban/current', finally the default
    board.  '<root>' is '$HERMES_KANBAN_HOME' or '~/.hermes'; a named board lives in
    '<root>/kanban/boards/<slug>/kanban.db'.  The first line of the summary names the board after
    the database it read: 'default', the slug of a named board, or 'custom' for anything else.

    Functions:

      hermeskanban_root - '$HERMES_KANBAN_HOME' or '~/.hermes'

      hermeskanban_db_default - the database the kernel would resolve for this caller

      hermeskanban_db_path <db option> <board option>
        the path the explicit options resolve to

      hermeskanban_board_name <board option>
        the board slug for the output header

      hermeskanban_sql <sql>
        run one query, print its rows with US separated fields
        '-readonly' keeps a board the caller may not write untouched; the second attempt covers a
        WAL database whose shared memory sidecar the kernel has not materialised yet.  A failure of
        both is a failure to read the board, which the caller turns into exit code 3.

      hermeskanban_quote <value>
        a SQL single quoted literal

      hermeskanban_in_list <value..>
        a quoted, comma joined SQL literal list

      hermeskanban_project_id <id-or-slug>
        the project id, or the argument unchanged
        '<root>/projects.db' maps a slug to its id; without it (or without that project) the
        argument is taken as an id verbatim, so a board database alone is enough to filter.

      hermeskanban_json <text>
        sets HERMESKANBAN_JSON to <text> as a JSON string literal
        Sets a global instead of printing: a command substitution would fork a subshell for every
        field of every record, which is what makes a listing of a hundred cards take a second
        (measured 800 ms for 90 cards before this, 90 ms after).  The escaping and the 'into a
        variable' form are 'json_set_string'; what stays local is kanban's own policy -- every
        control character becomes a space instead of being dropped, so a title keeps its word
        boundaries ('title with\na newline' reads 'title with a newline').

      hermeskanban_json_number_or_null <number|''>
        sets HERMESKANBAN_JSON to a JSON number, or 'null'
        The argument shape is kanban's (a run id or a duration as sqlite3 prints it, '' for none),
        the literal is 'json_set_number'.

      hermeskanban_duration <seconds>
        '3d4h', '5h12m', '12m30s' or '45s'

      hermeskanban_stamp <epoch>
        a local 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS', '-' for 0

      hermeskanban_truncate <text> <max>
        <text>, shortened to <max> bytes with a '...' tail

      hermeskanban_card_sql <where clauses..>
        the SELECT of one card record

      hermeskanban_card_field <record> <index, 1 based>
        one field of a card record

      hermeskanban_card_reason <kind> <reason> <error>
        why a blocked card is blocked

      hermeskanban_card_line <record> <reason budget, 0 = full>
        one card as one text line
        The record is split once, here: a 'read' per field would fork a subshell per field and cost
        a hundred milliseconds over a listing (measured on a 90 card answer).

      hermeskanban_gather_cards <where clauses..>
        load every matching card into HERMESKANBAN_ROWS

      hermeskanban_card_exists <task-id>
        0 when the card is on the board

      hermeskanban_write_all <suffix> <budget>
        write every record, print the path of the file
        The complete, untruncated answer: the convention is that whatever the screen leaves out is
        retrievable from a file whose path is printed next to the elision counts.  Written through a
        temporary name so a reader never sees a half written file.

      hermeskanban_record_line <record> <budget>
        one record through the active renderer

      hermeskanban_status_count <status>
        the count of one status, 0 when it is not a status

      hermeskanban_list_view <json> <limit>
        the matching cards, oldest of one priority first

      hermeskanban_list_json <printed> <limit>
        the listing as one JSON object

      hermeskanban_runs_sql <task-id>
        the SELECT of one run record

      hermeskanban_gather_runs <task-id>
        load the runs of a card into HERMESKANBAN_ROWS

      hermeskanban_run_line <record> <budget>
        one run as one text line

      hermeskanban_runs_view <task-id> <json> <limit>
        the runs of one card, newest first

      hermeskanban_runs_json <task-id> <status> <kind> <path> <branch> <title> <printed> <limit>

      hermeskanban_help - the usage text, on stdout

      hermeskanban_usage <message>
        the failure report, on stderr
        RULE_ARGS are the positional parameters of this function: the rule body passes them on.

    Rules:

      hermeskanban
        The bounded board state digest.  See the module header for the exit codes, the board resolution
        and the elision rules; nothing but the answer is written to stdout.

  init - Module to install, initialize and update bar itself

    Functions:

      init_install_modules <origin Bar.d> <target Bar.d>
        installs the modules every bar needs
        Only the modules which are loaded on every startup: 'std_lib' and 'rule_lib' (both
        required by bar itself) and the '*_rules'.  Everything else is vendored on demand when a
        rule first names it, which is what 'init_deps' does right after the installation.

      init_deps - completes a vendored 'Bar.d' with the modules the Barf really needs
        'bar init' installs the mandatory modules only ('init_install_modules'), this walks the
        rules of the local 'Barf' and vendors every module they (transitively) require from the
        installation bar was run from.  Run it again after the Barf grew a dependency to pick up
        the modules it now needs.

      init_install [prefix]
        install links in [prefix] or '~/.local'.
        Bar will usually exist somewhere in a users home directory where it got checked
        out. Bar does rolling releases, it is continuously improved. To make this checked out
        version available for use this 'init_install' will create symlinks at the given
        [prefix] directory or in '~/.local' by default.
        For 'please' every module of 'Bar.d/' is symlinked into '~/.config/please'.  Running
        'init_install' again adds the symlinks of modules which are not linked yet and leaves the
        existing ones untouched, so a module which was added to 'Bar.d/' later becomes reachable
        without a manual symlink.

    Rules:

      init
        Initialize bar in a workdir. This copies the main 'bar' script, a default 'Barf' and the modules
        bar loads on every startup ('std_lib', 'rule_lib' and the '*_rules') to the current directory,
        then 'init_deps' installs the modules the new 'Barf' needs.  With '--no-install-deps' that last
        step is left out, so the 'Barf' can be customized first and 'bar init_deps' run afterwards to
        vendor exactly what the edited 'Barf' names.  Every other module is vendored from the
        installation the first time a rule names it, so a 'Bar.d/' which was trimmed on purpose keeps
        working.  When the workdir is a git repository then '.gitignore' is updated to exclude the files
        and directories 'bar' will use for temporary/testing storage.  A user may edit the 'Barf' file to
        suit the project/personal preference, run 'init_deps' after that and delete modules from 'Bar.d/'
        which are not needed ('bar modules_prune' deletes the ones which are still unmodified copies of
        the installation's).  When that is done the new/changed files should be committed to version
        control.

      init_deps
        Install the modules the current 'Barf' needs into the vendored 'Bar.d'.  Rules which name a
        module (as a dependency or in a 'require' line of their body) get it vendored from the
        installation bar was run from, transitively.  Modules which are already there are left
        untouched, this only ever adds.

      init_update_existing
        Updates a initialized working tree. That is 'bar' itself will be updated when a newer
        version is available and any module in 'Bar.d/' that exists in the local working tree will
        be updated as well.  Barf will be left untouched.

      init_update
        Updates a initialized working tree. That is 'bar' itself will be updated when a newer
        version is available and all modules including ones that are not present in 'Bar.d/' will
        be updated as well.  Barf will be left untouched.

  json_lib - The JSON literals of bar's machine readable output: one escaping rule for every '--json' form.
    
    The JSON forms of the helpers -- 'hermeskanban --json', 'rad_state --json', 'sourcetree_state
    --json', 'hermes_logs --json', 'helpers --json', the report of 'bar check' -- are assembled from
    string, number, boolean and array literals.  Writing one of them is one small piece of policy
    and it belongs in one place:
    
      json_string <text>             the JSON string literal of <text>
      json_set_string <var> <text>   the same, left in <var> instead of printed
      json_number <value>            <value> as a JSON number, 'null' when it is no number
      json_set_number <var> <value>  the same, left in <var>
      json_bool <text>               'true' for 'yes', 'false' for 'no', 'null' otherwise
      json_list <text> [<sep>]       <text> cut at <sep> (default ','), as a JSON array of strings
      json_array <literal..>         the literals as one JSON array
    
    THE ESCAPING RULE
    
    Backslash and double quote are escaped, a tab becomes the escape '\t' and every other control
    character is dropped; nothing else about <text> changes.  A literal therefore never carries a
    raw control character, so one record is one line: an object can be held in a shell variable,
    compared byte for byte and cut by line without a parser.  Dropping is deliberate -- a newline
    inside a title would otherwise end the record.
    
    'EMPTY IS NULL'
    
    A field bar could not answer is 'null', not an empty string and not a zero: 'json_number'
    answers 'null' for anything which is no number (an empty value included), and 'json_bool'
    answers 'null' for a value which is neither 'yes' nor 'no', so an unset flag stays
    distinguishable from a flag which is off.  An empty array is a different answer and stays '[]':
    'no labels' is a fact.
    
    WHY THE SETTERS EXIST
    
    A command substitution forks a subshell, and a listing quotes a dozen fields per record:
    'hermeskanban' measured 800 ms for 90 cards when every field forked and 90 ms when none did.
    A caller which quotes many fields asks for the literal in a variable with the setters and never
    forks; a caller with one or two fields asks for the printed form.  Both forms are one
    implementation -- 'json_string' calls 'json_set_string' -- so the rule above cannot drift.
    
    This module is a library: no rules, no helpers and therefore no helper module header of its own
    (a lib is not a repertoire module, see Bar.d/helpers_rules).  A module whose '--json' form needs
    literal says 'require json_lib'; where a field is not a JSON literal but module policy -- the
    yes/no/unset rule of rad, the flag list of sourcetree, the control characters 'hermeskanban'
    replaces by a space instead of dropping them -- that policy stays in the module, which asks
    these functions for the literal and keeps only the decision.

    Functions:

      json_set_string <var> <text>
        put the JSON string literal of <text> into <var>
        The escaping rule of every '--json' form of bar.  Backslash and quote first (so the escapes
        they introduce are not escaped again), then the tab -- which is the one control character
        worth carrying, an indented line is common in a log -- and finally every other control
        character, dropped with the same pattern substitution 'hermeskanban' uses: no 'tr', so no
        subshell per field.

      json_string <text>
        a JSON string literal

      json_set_number <var> <value>
        put <value> as a JSON number into <var>, 'null' if none

      json_number <value>
        <value> as a JSON number, 'null' when there is none

      json_bool <text>
        'true', 'false' or 'null' for a yes/no/unset field

      json_list <text> [<sep>]
        the <sep> separated <text> as a JSON array of strings
        The members are the fields of <text>, trimmed; an empty member is dropped, so an unset field
        is '[]' and not '[""]'.  The separator is '+' for the flag lists of a digest and ',' for
        everything a user wrote.

      json_array <literal..>
        the literals as one JSON array

  lock - Library for managing lockfiles

    Functions:

      lock_wait <lockname>
        Waits until we have the lock on a lockfile.
        '.lock' is automatically appended to the 'lockname'.
        These locks are recursive the same process can lock_wait on the same lockfile multiple
        times which must be paired with the same numbers of 'lock_remove' to unlock it.

      lock_try_norec <lockname>
        try to lock a lockfile non recursively.
        Will not wait, fails when we already have the lock
        Locks 'new' and then removes the 'old' locck.

      lock_send <lock> <who>
        Sends a 'lock' to another process with pid 'who'.
        Locks can be send atomically to another process. 'lock_send' registers the lock to be
        send to a process, the lock will be send at the final lock_remove in the sending
        process when all recursive locks are freed. Note that to obtain the 'who' pid the
        receiver has to be started first.

      lock_receive <lock>
        Wait for lock send by another process.
        Blocks until the the sender removed all uses of 'lock',

      lock_remove <lock>
        Unlocks 'lock'.
        'lock_remove' must be paired with the same numbers of earlier 'lock_wait'. Only the
        last 'lock_remove' will release the lockfile.

  memodb - A persistent database which memoizes command results
    
    It records the stdout, stderr and state/exitcode commands and functions.  Commands and
    functions that are used with the memodb must be pure as their result must only depend on
    the arguments passed and the directory they are called in. The content of the directory is
    hashed for the database. This requires properly set up '.gitignore' rules to ignore any
    build artifacts. Otherwise subsequent instances will not use the same memodb and re-run
    commands.
    
    This is used for
     1. Persist/cache command results throughout multiple invocations
     2. Implement background processing where one invocation can schedule commands to be
        executed in the background and a later invocation can collect the results

    Variables:

      MEMODB_KEEP (gx)
        How many trees to keep.
        Default: ${MEMODB_KEEP:-5}

      MEMODB_BACKGROUNDING (gx)
        Global backgrounding flag.
        Default: ${MEMODB_BACKGROUNDING:-true}

    Functions:

      memodb_eval <cmd> [args..]
        Execute a command in foreground and stores its results.
        The command is only executed on the first time the 'memodb_eval' is called with the
        same options in the same directory. Any subsequent call will replay the stored results.

      memodb_schedule <cmd> [args..]
        Schedules a command to be run in background.
        Will 'memodb_eval' the command when 'MEMODB_BACKGROUNDING' is not true.

      memodb_result <cmd> [args..]
        Retrieves the background results.
        Waits for the backgrounding to be completed. Merges the results into the current memodb.
        Replays the result (stdout/stderr/exitcode) of the given 'cmd [args..]'.
        'cmd [args..]' must be the same as used when scheduling it to the background.
        Will error when the command was not previously scheduled and backgrounding is enabled.

  modules - Module to prune the unmodified copies out of a vendored 'Bar.d'
    
    'bar init' and 'require' vendor the modules a project uses from the installation bar was run
    from, so a project's 'Bar.d' grows with it and a module stays behind when the rules which named
    it are dropped from the 'Barf'.  'modules_prune' removes those copies and completes the tree
    again with 'init_deps'.
    
    The name is what the module autoloader resolves the command to (a rule loads the module named by
    the word before its first '_'), so this lives here and not in the 'init' module which vendors
    and completes the tree.

    Functions:

      modules_standard <module>
        true for the modules a vendored 'Bar.d' always carries
        The modules 'init_install_modules' installs unconditionally, because they are loaded on
        every startup: 'std_lib' and 'rule_lib' (both required by bar itself) and the '*_rules'.
        'modules_prune' never removes them, everything else is vendored on demand.

      modules_prune [--dry-run]
        removes the unmodified vendored modules, then completes the tree again
        'bar init' vendors the modules a project needs from the installation bar was run from
        (:func:`init_deps`) and 'require' vendors the ones it starts to use later (see
        :func:`bar_vendor_modules` in the bar script), so a project's 'Bar.d' grows with it.  A
        module which is not needed anymore -- the rules which named it were dropped from the 'Barf'
        -- stays behind.  This removes every module which is a byte identical copy of the one in the
        origin and runs :func:`init_deps` afterwards, which vendors back the modules the current
        'Barf' really needs.  What stays is the modules the project uses plus the ones it customized.
        A module is only ever removed when the origin has one of the same name and the two are
        identical.  A customized module (the content to preserve), a module the origin does not have
        at all (it was written here) and one which is not a regular file (a link into the origin)
        are left alone, and so are the standard modules (:func:`modules_standard`).
        With [--dry-run] the modules which would be removed are reported and nothing is written,
        'init_deps' does not run either.  Returns non-zero when there is no origin to compare with,
        that is when the 'Bar.d' in use is the installation's own.

    Rules:

      modules_prune
        Removes every module of a vendored 'Bar.d' which is an unmodified copy of the installation's one
        and then completes the tree again with 'init_deps': what only the dropped rules needed
        disappears, a customized module and one the origin does not have are preserved.  Modules are
        compared byte for byte, only the installation's own modules can be removed, never anything the
        project wrote.  With '--dry-run' nothing is removed and 'init_deps' does not run.

  net - Low level networking functions

    Functions:

      net_canon_hostname <host>
        Canonalize 'host' to a FQDN.

      net_host_reachable <host> [timeout [tries]]
        checks if 'host' is reachable.
        The check uses ICMP ping to check for reachability.
        The [timeout] defaults to 10 seconds and [tries] defaults to 3.

      net_wakeonlan <host>
        Sends a wake-on-lan signal.
        'host' is a hostname. '/etc/ethers' must have entries to resolve hosts. See
        'contrib/update-ethers.sh' for automatically discover and maintain '/etc/ethers'. Waits
        for the host to become reachable. Fails when it is not reachable.

  rad - The radicle domain of bar: what this checkout is, what it carries, whether the author of an
    incoming item may be trusted, how a topic starts -- and how a repository gets onto radicle in
    the first place.
    
    The helpers, all served by the backing library Bar.d/radicle_lib (running 'rad', reading its
    padded tables back, the age of a last event, the node storage, the '.worktrees/' exclusion and
    the JSON literals):
    
      bar rad_state [--json] [--max <n>]                     the whole state, one screen
      bar rad_patches [--state <s>] [--author <a>] [--json] [--max <n>]
                                                             only the patches, filtered
      bar rad_trust --repo <rid> --author <who> [--grounds <..>] [--dump] [--second-party]
                                                             which grounds of trust that author has
      bar rad_comment_metadata <item> --query <label|assign|unassign>
                                                             the trusted '*label:'/'*assign:'/
                                                             '*unassign:' proposals of one item
      bar rad_topic <class> "<description>"                  open the topic tree of an item
      bar rad_reaction [--from <emoji>] <to> <target>..      set/transition/withdraw our own marker
      bar rad_cob <list|ops|document> ..                    what the COB store holds, one chain,
                                                             one document -- one interface for a
                                                             consumer that must not know the layout
      bar rad_rm_label <item> <glob>..                       remove the stored labels a glob matches
      bar rad_import                                         import this repository into radicle
      bar rad_merge_accepted_prs [--dry-run] [--no-fetch] [--max <n>]
                                                             merge the patches a delegate accepted
      bar rad_rebase_ask [--after <patch>] [--branch <branch>] [--onto <sha>] [--dry-run]
                                                             ask the pending patches of a branch to
                                                             re-cut themselves after a merge landed
    
    Every helper answers '-h' or '--help' with its usage on stdout and exit 0.
    
    'rad_state' answers the questions an agent asks before it touches a radicle repository: which
    repository is this (RID, name, path), which identity does 'rad' use here, how the patches stand
    (how many, by state, ours against others), how the issues stand (state, labels, when something
    happened last) and which local branches still carry commits no patch exposes.  'rad_patches' is
    the patch section of that report, filtered by state and by author.
    
    Nothing there goes to the network: 'rad patch/issue list' answer from the local node storage and
    the divergence is measured against 'rad/main' as the last 'git fetch rad' left it, which the
    'head' line names.  Syncing is the caller's business ('rad sync', 'git fetch rad'): this helper
    reports what is there and says how old it is.  'rad_topic' is the one exception -- it fetches the
    radicle remote (else 'origin') to *know* how the base relates to upstream, see below.
    'rad_merge_accepted_prs' is the other exception to the "nothing there goes to the network"
    reading: it fetches 'rad' first, because the branch a patch has to be merged into has to be
    the current one.  It is also the one helper which writes to a canonical branch -- the one of
    the checkout it runs in -- which is why it refuses under an identity which is not a delegate
    of that repository, and why '--dry-run' exists for every other identity.  The acceptance it
    acts on is read off the patch's own chain, never off 'rad patch list': a Reviews column cannot
    say which revision a verdict names, and an acceptance of an older revision is exactly what
    must not be acted on.
    
    SOURCES
    
      rad inspect --rid         the repository identity, 'git rev-parse' the toplevel
      rad self                  alias and DID of the identity 'rad' uses here
      rad patch list --all      patch id, title, author and head (the state is not in the list)
      rad issue list --all      issue id, title, author, labels and assignees
      rad patch/issue list --authored/--draft/--merged/--archived/--closed/--solved
                                rad prints one state per call, so the states are subtracted as
                                sets: 'open' is what is in '--all' and in none of the others, and
                                'ours' is what '--authored' (patches) or '(you)' (issues) says
      the node storage          time and kind of the last event of every patch and issue, read
                                from 'refs/namespaces/*/refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.<type>' below
                                '$(rad path)/storage/<RID>' with 'git for-each-ref'
      git for-each-ref/rev-list the current branch, the local branches and their distance from
                                'refs/remotes/rad/main' or 'refs/remotes/rad/master'
      git ls-remote/log/branch  'rad_topic': the trunk on both sides and the commits in between
    
    OUTPUT
    
      Text: one header line per section -- '<section>  <counts>' -- then one line per item, the
      free text last so that nothing has to be padded.  Ages are short ('6m', '2h', '1y') and are
      those of the last event.  '--json': one object marked with 'bar_rad': 1, the complete data,
      never elided.
      At most '--max' items per section (default 10, '--max 0' lists all).  A section which is
      cut states exactly how much ('n of N elided'), and when anything was cut the complete text
      is written to a file whose path the last line prints, so the elision loses nothing.
    
      'ours' is what 'rad' itself calls ours, everything else is 'other' (whose author is then
      named behind the title); a patch which rad lists in more than one state is named by the
      first of 'draft', 'merged', 'archived'.
    
    EXIT CODES
    
      0  answered (an empty repository -- no patch, no issue -- is an answer, not a failure)
      2  usage error
      3  no data: not a git repository, or no radicle repository here
      1  could not answer: 'rad' is missing or failed, or its output was not understood
    
    'rad_trust' answers whether the author of an incoming issue or patch counts as a *trusted
    source* for the triage policy (`incoming-issue-triage` §3) -- and, above that, **which grounds of
    trust** that author has, so the caller decides per action instead of paying for one fixed rule.
    The grounds are a disjoint list (any one of them suffices, an implied OR, no expressions):
    
      delegate      the author is a delegate of this repository
      owner         the author is the identity which established the repository
      opened        the author opened the item under discussion
      assigned      the author is an assignee of that item
      contributed   the commits the identity authored as itself reach the threshold
    
    'owner' and the two item grounds are facts about the repository's own copy and its item, so they
    need '--issue <id>' or '--patch <id>'; without an item they are 'false', never an error.
    
      bar rad_trust --repo rad:z3WhBdHt… --author cehteh
      trusted author=cehteh delegate=yes authored=396 threshold=10 ground=delegate reason=delegate of this repository repo=rad:z3WhBdHt1VVNyeQ61zpY66pNzuSrP
    
      bar rad_trust --repo rad:z3WhBdHt… --author cehteh --grounds delegate,assigned --issue d896735
      bar rad_trust --repo rad:z3WhBdHt… --author cehteh --dump --issue d896735    every ground, no verdict
      bar rad_trust --repo rad:z3WhBdHt… --author cehteh --grounds contributed --threshold 10%
    
    '--grounds' names the grounds which suffice (default 'delegate,contributed', the one rule this
    helper answered before), and the 'ground' it reports is the first of them which holds, in the order
    they were listed.  '--dump' answers every ground and evaluates *nothing*: no verdict, no threshold,
    exit 0.  That is the call a caller makes once per scope and then caches -- the grounds are values,
    the decision is the caller's -- so a second call in the same scope is wasted work.
    
    '--second-party' answers the one question an approval asks: is this actor somebody *other* than
    the identity which opened the item -- the second party a consent needs.  It is the dump above
    plus one field, 'second_party': 'false' when the actor opened the item, 'true' otherwise.  A
    verdict about no item has no opener to compare against, so it needs '--issue <id>' or
    '--patch <id>' and is a usage error (2) without one.  Whatever the grounds say, our own identity
    answering on an item it opened is not a second party.
    
    '--threshold' decides 'contributed' alone: a bare number counts commits (default 10), a percent
    ('10%', '0.5%') is the share of the commits every identity authored in this repository, which is
    what lets one setting mean the same in a big and in a small one.  The numbers come from the fact
    tool 'RAD_TRIAGE_FACTS' names (delegates from the identity document, the commits an identity
    authored as its own NID from the COB commits, plus the code commits of the humans behind its
    patches); the grounds about the repository's own copy -- owner, opened, assigned and the total the
    share is measured against -- are read from the node storage, and only when a ground asks for them.
    The '--json' form is the whole object the tool answered plus 'grounds', and in verdict mode
    'verdict', 'ground' and 'reason' as well.
    
      exit 0 trusted -- 1 untrusted -- 2 could not answer (unknown author, no data, usage)
    
    'rad_comment_metadata' is the second half of the same policy, for the writes nobody but a
    delegate may make.  Radicle authorizes an issue's `Label`, `Assign` and `Lifecycle` actions to
    the repository delegates (and to the issue's author for editing and closing it) -- verified in
    the implementation (`heartwood`, 'crates/radicle/src/cob/issue.rs', 'Issue::authorization'), so
    an incoming contributor *cannot* label or assign anything: the store refuses it.  Instead the
    proposal travels as a **structured comment**: the first lines of a comment may be
    
      *label:improvement
      *assign:cehteh
      *unassign:ct_vibes
    
    one per line, starting at the comment (the issue description counts -- rad calls it the root
    comment of the thread).  This helper reads those blocks back from the node storage and returns
    only the entries a trusted source wrote:
    
      bar rad_comment_metadata <item> [--query <key>].. [--repo <rid|path>] [--trust <spec>].. [--last] [--json]
    
    The form is '*<key>:<value>' and the vocabulary is open: a key is a word of letters, digits, '_'
    and '-' which starts with a letter, and *any* key is read back -- what a key means is the
    caller's business, and only the keys with a registered effect ('label', 'rmlabel', 'assign',
    'unassign') are ever applied (see the write layer below).  The block ends at the first line which
    is not a marker, so a marker quoted inside a reply is prose.  A **value runs to the end of the
    line** and may carry spaces: what a value may hold belongs to the directive that owns the key, so
    the reader hands the line out as it is and only the label effect normalises it (a label is a word
    without whitespace -- radicle writes a space as '-', and so does 'rad_label_name').  '--query'
    names the keys to answer for and repeats; with no '--query' at all the answer carries every key a
    comment proposes, and every line names its own key.
    
    '<item>' is the issue or patch the question is about, short or full -- one item only, not the
    whole repository: the ref listing is a single call, the chains (the ops and their payloads) are
    opened for that COB alone.  An id the node does not hold is 'no data' (3), an id an issue and a
    patch both match is a usage error (2).
    
    '--trust' takes 'did:<did|nid>' (that identity, named) and 'contributor(<n>)' (an identity with
    at least <n> authored commits); with no '--trust' only the **delegates** are trusted, which is
    the narrow default.  '--last' keeps the newest block *per key* -- the last record of the chain
    order, not of the hash order: two markers written in the same second are ordered by where they
    sit in the chain (parent before child), which is what makes 'the newest block' mean what it says.
    A block which is the newest word on two keys answers for both, and a newer block nobody can be
    attributed to is skipped, so an older trusted block still applies.
    The trust facts come from the same tool 'rad_trust' uses ('tools/radicle_triage/triage-facts').
    A delegate's maintenance command reads the trusted proposals back and applies them -- the labels
    and assignments themselves still need that delegate's own key.
    
    The write layer of the same policy.  One function per effect, each of them exactly one 'rad'
    invocation, and none of them asks about trust: that is the reader's decision, which is why the
    reader is the only way in.  These are not rules -- they are the API a rule (or the daemon the
    project builds elsewhere) composes, which is why they are named like the plumbing they are:
    
      bar rad_set_label <item> [<add>] [<delete>]..    one 'rad issue|patch label' write
      bar rad_assign <item> <did|alias>                one 'rad issue|patch assign' add
      bar rad_unassign <item> <did|alias>              one 'rad issue|patch assign' delete
      bar rad_apply_from_comments <item>.. [--query <key>].. [--repo <rid|path>] [--trust <spec>]..
                                 [--dry-run]           the trusted proposals of an item, applied
    
    '<item>' is the form the reader answers with ('issue:<id>', 'patch:<id>'); a bare id is an issue.
    The identity of an assignment is resolved before it is written -- a comment cannot be trusted to
    spell a DID right -- by the same fact command the trust gate asks, and a value which resolves to
    nothing is refused rather than written as it stands.  Every applier reports what it applied
    ('applied label item=issue:4444444 add=idea delete=wip') and no applier writes into the thread.
    
    'rad_apply_from_comments' reads the newest trusted block per key (that is what applies; a block a
    later one superseded is history) and dispatches every entry to the applier of its key: 'label',
    'rmlabel', 'assign' and 'unassign'.  Any other key is reported and never guessed at.  An entry
    whose author cannot be attributed never reaches it -- the reader drops those, silently, like every
    proposal which is not trusted.  A label the item already says is not written again ('skip when the
    labels already say it'), and '--dry-run' prints what would be applied without writing anything.
    Nothing is remembered between calls: what the item carries is read fresh, from 'rad issue|patch
    list', every time.
    
    The labels are *one* state, and the colon decides which of them the applier maintains by itself.
    A label carrying a ':' is namespaced -- the 'pr:<id>'/'issue:<id>' link labels and every other
    '<namespace>:<rest>' -- and it is therefore **protected**: a chain transition never deletes it,
    and it leaves the item only when a '*rmlabel:' glob or a direct write names it.  The colon-free
    labels are the *chain slot*, and exactly one of those is kept: the delete set of a transition is
    the stored colon-free labels, and the new one goes in with the old one out in the same write.
    (Before the colon decided this, *every* stored label the newest block did not name was deleted,
    which is what ate the link labels.)  A stored label no proposal names is reported and left alone
    -- deleting on the strength of an absent proposal would reverse a delegate's write, and a
    delegate may label by hand with no comment at all.
    
    '*rmlabel:<glob>' is the explicit removal, and it runs *before* the labels of the same block: the
    glob is matched against the whole stored label, anchored at both ends ('pr:*' takes 'pr:<id>' and
    never 'xpr:<id>', '*' takes everything), a glob which matches nothing writes nothing and says so
    (exit 0 -- nothing to remove is an answer), and the removal has to come first so that a block
    carrying both a '*rmlabel:' and a '*label:' in the same namespace ends with the new label in.
    
    'rad_reaction' is the same idea for the reaction markers, and those are a different animal: a
    reaction is *private per identity* and can never be delegated, so there is no trust question here
    -- what the caller compares and swaps is its *own* state, and that state is read from 'rad cob
    show --format json': the store's own reduction of the COB, whose per-type shapes are unified here
    into one list of the live markers of ours on the target.  A transition writes the marker to set
    beside the one it takes back (one op, two actions), which is the shape the reduction is read
    against; a reader reducing the op chain itself cannot tell a marker from one its author took back
    in the very op next to it, and reports a superseded marker as live for ever (bar issue 9a815de).
    
    The state of one target is a *set*: rad keeps one entry per emoji, so the same marker cannot be
    live twice, while two *different* markers of ours can be.  One call is still one marker's business:
    '<from>' names exactly one, the comparison asks only whether that one *is* live (never that it is
    the whole state), and the step takes it back and sets '<to>'.  A second marker standing there is
    left where it is, and a '<to>' which is already live is a no-op add.  'active' is a value and never
    a toggle: a marker is taken back by its own action, not by writing it a second time.  The read is
    the *current* state only -- 'rad cob show'
    takes no '--from'/'--until' -- which is what a caller needs before it writes; the state at some
    point in time is 'rad cob log --from/--until' instead.
    
      bar rad_reaction [--from <emoji>] [--repo <rid|path>] <to> <target>..
      bar rad_reaction <from> <to> <target>.. [--repo <rid|path>]
    
    '<to>' is the marker to set: one emoji of the store's allow list, checked before any call, so the
    '❤️' an emoji picker hands out (heart plus a variation selector) is refused here instead of by the
    store.  A target is '<issue-id>' (the issue itself), '<issue-id> <comment-id>',
    '<patch-or-revision-id>' (a patch id is its root revision) or '<patch-or-revision-id> <id>' -- the
    second id being any reaction-able part of the patch: a comment of the revision, a review, or a
    comment of a review.
    
    '<from>' is the one marker the caller expects to be live there: omitted is no expectation at all,
    the empty string is 'no live marker of mine', anything else must be live -- and an expectation which
    does not hold fails without writing anything.  Exactly one marker, because the step changes exactly
    one: it takes '<from>' back and sets '<to>' in the same op, so any other marker standing on the
    target is none of this call's business, and a '<to>' which is already live costs nothing.  A value
    naming several markers ('--from "✅ ❓"') is therefore a usage error, not a state.  The action list
    carries the marker to set first and the withdrawal after it, and rad lands that list as a single op
    -- in one op the actions apply in order and the last one wins, which is why the marker to set comes
    first.  Its '<from>' is gone and its '<to>' is live afterwards; any other marker stays.  The typed
    commands are
    deliberately not used -- 'rad issue react' cannot take a marker back at all ('--undo' is patch only
    in rad 1.10.3) and the patch commands would be a second code path for the same effect.  An empty
    '<to>' takes the named markers back and sets nothing, and it needs '--from', because the
    expectation is what names the markers.
    
    'rad_topic' opens the topic tree of that work in one action: it mints the name from the
    branch *class* and the description, resolves the clone and the freshest base, keeps '.worktrees/'
    out of the main checkout's status and runs 'git worktree add' -- the sequence an agent otherwise
    reassembles by hand every time it starts a topic:
    
      bar rad_topic fix "crash on empty config"
      <clone>/.worktrees/fix-crash-on-empty-config  fix-crash-on-empty-config  base=rad/main  head=55d5924
    
    The name is '<class>-<description>'.  The class vocabulary is read from the branch matchers of
    the 'git' module ('is_git_*_branch'), so bar's own classes and the gates it hangs off them stay
    the one source of truth, and a topic's name says what kind of change it is from anywhere -- the
    worktree, the branch and the patch head.  A name without a class is refused, never guessed.
    <description> is slugified to '[a-z0-9._-]' and the whole name stays within 40 characters, the
    limit the Hermes worktree machinery sanitises to; a description which cannot fit is refused, never
    silently cut.
    
    The clone is the *main checkout* of the repository '--root' names, or the one the current
    directory is in -- a linked worktree resolves to its clone, so the call works from inside any
    topic.  The base is the clone's *trunk*: on this host the trunk is upstream plus the local work
    which is not published yet, so branching off the remote tip would drop that work.  The 'rad'
    remote (else 'origin') is therefore fetched first -- to *know* the relation -- and a trunk which
    is behind upstream is refused, because updating the trunk is its own step with its own gate and
    never a side effect of opening a topic.  '--base <ref>' overrides the base, '--no-fetch' skips
    the fetch.
    
    Neither of the two rules above writes to the main checkout: the tree is created below
    '<clone>/.worktrees/', kept out of 'git status' by a line in '.git/info/exclude' (never by the
    tracked '.gitignore'), and 'git worktree add' does not touch the trunk's HEAD.
    
    'rad_import' is the way in, not a question: on the toplevel of a git repository which is not on
    radicle yet it runs 'rad init' (public, signing set up) and pushes every branch to the new
    remote.  Once a 'rad' remote exists it does nothing but say so.
    
    Running 'rad', reading its tables back, the age of a last event and the COB store are shared with
    every other module of bar which talks to radicle and live in Bar.d/radicle_lib, which this module
    requires: 'RAD_BIN' (the command to run instead of 'rad'), the record separators and every 'rad_*'
    function come from there.  The JSON literals of the '--json' forms below come from
    Bar.d/json_lib, the one place where bar writes them.
    
    'RAD_STATE_MAX' is the default of '--max'.  'RAD_TRIAGE_FACTS' names the fact tool 'rad_trust'
    reads from: the path of the triage fact tool of the caller's own installation, and the only
    setting for it -- bar cannot know the layout of another machine, so there is no default, and an
    unset or unexecutable setting is named and answered with 'could not answer' (exit 2) instead of
    a silently wrong verdict.  Reading rad's tables back needs a character aware shell; bar exports a
    UTF-8 'LC_ALL' whenever the system has one.
    
    The rules below are about 'rad', the radicle frontend and the repository it is used in.  Building
    a radicle installation itself is not one of them (radicle is packaged; the tools below
    'tools/radicle_*' are their own thing).

    Variables:

      RAD_STATE_MAX (gx)
        default of '--max': items printed per section
        Default: ${RAD_STATE_MAX:-10}

      RAD_STATE_PATCHES (ga)
        one record per patch, see 'rad_state_collect'
        Default: ()

      RAD_STATE_ISSUES (ga)
        one record per issue, see 'rad_state_collect'
        Default: ()

      RAD_STATE_BRANCHES (ga)
        one record per local branch, see 'rad_state_collect'
        Default: ()

      RAD_STATE_COB_TIME (gA)
        '<type>:<id>' -> '<updated> <created> <kind>'
        Default: ()

      RAD_STATE_CUT (gi)
        items the last text rendering elided
        Default: 0

      RAD_STATE_NOW (gi)
        the timestamp the ages are relative to
        Default: 0

      RAD_TRUST_STORAGE (g)
        the node storage of it, once it is resolved

      RAD_TRUST_ITEM_REF (g)
        the COB of the item asked about, once it is resolved

      RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_AUTHORED (gA)
        '<rid>|<nid>' -> the commits that identity authored
        Default: ()

      RAD_METADATA_AUTHORED_VALUE (g)
        what 'rad_metadata_authored' hands back

      RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_ASSIGNEES (ga)
        the current assignees of the item asked about
        Default: ()

    Functions:

      rad_import import a git repository into radicle
        'rad' pads the cells of its tables by character, so the columns can only be cut at the same
        offsets when the shell measures the same way.  That is the case in a UTF-8 locale, which bar
        exports whenever the system has one; in a byte locale a cell holding a non ASCII character
        shifts everything behind it and 'rad_state' says so instead of reporting it wrongly.
        (A double width character -- CJK, emoji -- is one column to a character wise shell and two
        to rad: such a title would shift the columns of its row too.)
        A state filter is answered with a table of the same shape and only the ids are taken from
        it.  An empty result is 'Nothing to show.' or nothing at all -- that is no id, not an
        error; box drawing characters without a header to cut by are an error (return 1).
        'Nothing to show.' and nothing at all are no rows, not an error; box drawing characters
        without a header to cut by are.
          patch  id state own author head title updated created kind
          issue  id state own author labels assignees title updated created kind
          branch name commit ahead patch
        Returns 3 when there is no repository to report on (a radicle repository is a git
        repository with a radicle identity), 1 when rad failed or its output was not understood.
        the author of a patch of ours is us, naming it again would only repeat the identity
        line; for the others it says who to talk to
        'rad_state_usage' reports to stderr for a usage error; as the answer to '--help' the
        same text is the output, so its stream is folded over.

      rad_classes - the branch classes bar defines, one '<class>-' prefix per line
        Read from the 'is_git_*_branch' rules of the git module, so the vocabulary has one source of
        truth: the matcher 'fix-* fix/*' contributes the class 'fix-'.  Bar's matches are regexes, so
        'fix-*' is the prefix 'fix-' followed by anything; the slash forms ('fix/', 'release/') are
        not classes of a *topic* here -- this host's layout puts a task id behind the slash
        ('<slug>/<task-id>') -- and the bare trunks ('main', 'master', 'devel') have no prefix at all.

      rad_slug <text>
        the description as a name slug, empty when nothing is left

      rad_trust_ground_known <name>
        0 when <name> is one of the grounds

      rad_trust_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr
        Which repository, and where its COBs are: every ground which is a fact about the node's own copy
        ('owner', 'opened', 'assigned') and a relative threshold need it -- the two facts the fact tool
        answers do not, and resolving it costs a 'rad' invocation, so it happens only when a ground asks
        for it.  A repository the node does not hold is *could not answer* (2): nothing about it can be
        established, not even 'false'.
        The 'owner' ground.  Radicle has no owner field: a repository belongs to its delegates and the
        identity document (payload, delegates, threshold) names no single owner.  The one identity a
        'whose repository is this' question can be answered with is the identity that *established* it:
        the author of the initial revision of the identity document, which is the one 'rad init' gave
        the project to.  An identity document which is not there, or which has no history, answers
        nothing -- the ground is 'false' then, never an error.
        The denominator of a relative threshold: the commits authored as *some* identity
        ('<alias>@<NID>'), counted over all refs of the node's copy the same way the fact tool counts the
        author's own -- which is what lets one setting ('10%') mean the same in a big and in a small
        repository.  A count, never a failure: no commits means zero.
        The item the 'opened' and 'assigned' grounds are about, by its id (short or full) and of the type
        the caller named.  An id the node does not hold, or one which is the other type, is the caller's
        error and not 'false': a caller asking about an item this node cannot show cannot act on a no.
        The item's author is the author of the op which *created* it.  A COB is chained onto the identity
        commits -- and a patch COB onto the patch's own commits -- so the create op is found by its
        subject ('Create issue'/'Create patch') and confirmed by the manifest next to it, which names the
        item type: a foreign op in the same chain is not the create op, and an op whose manifest says
        otherwise is skipped rather than believed.

      rad_trust see the module header
        Which grounds are asked for: the named ones, or -- with no '--grounds' -- the rule this helper
        has always answered (a delegate, or the authored commits reaching the threshold).  '--dump'
        means all of them, and evaluates nothing.
        The tool's '--threshold' is an absolute count, so a relative threshold is not handed to it: that
        comparison is made here, from the same count and the repository's own total.  The tool's own
        'contributor' verdict is not used either -- the grounds are what decides, once.
        The tool exits non-zero when it cannot answer (an unresolvable author is one of those), and still
        prints the JSON which says why -- so the exit code is reported, never interpreted: an empty
        answer is 'could not answer', anything else is read and the reason travels on.
        One jq pass, so the text form and the machine form carry the same numbers.  The fields are joined
        with US (0x1f) and not with a tab: an empty field has to survive the read, and IFS whitespace
        would collapse it.
        An author the repository cannot resolve is *not* an error of the tool: it has no data, and that
        is 'unknown' (exit 2), never 'untrusted'.  Nothing can be established for it, so no ground is.
        'contributed': the commits this identity authored as itself against the threshold -- absolute, or
        as the share of the commits every identity authored in this repository.  The total is read when a
        relative threshold compares it, and in '--dump' (the cache call wants the share of a repository,
        not only of a relative threshold).
        'owner' and the two item grounds are facts about the node's own copy of the repository, so they
        need its storage -- resolved once, and only when one of them is asked for.
        No item named: the two item grounds cannot be established.  They are 'false', never an error
        -- a caller which asks about them without an item has nothing to decide either.
        The verdict: the first ground which holds **in the order the caller listed them**, so a caller
        lists its grounds in the order it wants them reported.

      rad_metadata_nid <did-or-nid>
        the bare NID of an identity
        'did:key:z6Mk…', 'did:z6Mk…' and a bare NID all mean the same identity; 'rad' prints the
        first form and a human types any of them.

      rad_metadata_mail_nid <mail>
        the NID 'rad' writes into a COB op's author mail
        A COB op is authored as '<alias>@<nid>' (measured: 'cehteh@z6MksaJk…'), so the *actor* of an
        op travels in that mail.  The namespace the op was read from does not identify it: one COB's
        chain is replicated into every namespace which holds it, byte for byte the same commits.

      rad_metadata_marks <body>
        the marker lines at the very start of <body>
        One '<key>US<value>' record per line of the block.  A marker is '*<key>:<value>' with the key
        a word of letters, digits, '_' and '-', starting with a letter -- the '<key>:<value>' header
        form and no fixed vocabulary: what a key *means* is the caller's business (this reads any key
        back, and only the keys with a registered effect are ever applied).  The block starts at the
        comment and ends at the first line which is not a marker: a marker quoted inside a reply is
        prose, not a proposal.
        A value runs to the **end of the line** and may contain spaces -- what a value may hold is the
        business of the directive that owns the key (a label follows radicle's rules, which is what
        'rad_label_name' is for; a trigger takes one of four words), so this reader hands the line out
        as it is instead of deciding for its callers.  Only the surrounding whitespace is trimmed: an
        empty value is no proposal and the block goes on, exactly like any other line.

      rad_label_name <value>
        the label a proposal's value names
        Radicle's own rule, applied where it belongs: what a label *is* is the applier's business, so
        the reader hands the line out whole and the label effect normalises the value.  A label is a
        non-empty string **without whitespace** (the store's 'Label::new': no length limit, no other
        rejection), and radicle writes a space as a '-', which is why a value carrying one is written
        the same way here -- each run of whitespace becomes one '-'.  So '*label:two words' proposes
        'two-words', the label a writer composing that directive is told to emit in the first place.

      rad_metadata_authored <rid> <nid>
        the commits that identity authored, '-' when unknown
        Asked once per identity and remembered, so several thresholds cost one lookup.
        Counted is only what that identity authored *as itself* ('<alias>@<nid>'): the gate has to
        resolve into a radicle identity, and commits of plain git authors carry no radicle identity
        and are therefore dropped.  That is why 'commits_as_identity' is read and not the wider
        'total_authored' -- an identity the command cannot resolve stays '-' and is not trusted.
        'RAD_TRUST_CMD' is provided by the deployment (a command and its arguments, e.g.
        'please rad_trust'): where the facts about an identity come from is not this module's
        business, and a home directory path does not belong into a vendored source file.
        An identity whose count cannot be read is not trusted -- say so, else a broken facts
        command looks exactly like a contributor who is below the threshold.

      rad_metadata_trust <rid> <nid>
        the verdict for one identity, in 'RAD_METADATA_TRUST'
        Empty when the identity is not trusted: a delegate, an identity named with '--trust did:…',
        one of the item's assignees, or an identity whose authored commits reach one of the
        'contributor(<n>)' thresholds.  An identity which is not of the radicle system at all ('z6M…')
        is never trusted -- there is nothing to authenticate such an op against.

      rad_metadata_body <storage> <op> <type> <subject>
        the body of one op of that COB
        Only the op kinds which can carry a body are opened ('Comment', 'Edit comment', 'Create
        issue', 'Edit root'), and the payload is the blob 'rad' names '0'.  An op of another COB can
        stand in this chain (rad chains a COB onto the identity commits), so the op has to declare the
        type the ref carries -- else a foreign comment would be reported against this item.
        Returns 1 when this op carries no body of that type; the caller skips it either way.

      rad_comment_metadata_ref <storage> <item>
        the one COB the item names
        The ref listing is a single call and it is what says which COB the id names.  A COB lives in
        every namespace which replicated it, so it is keyed by type and id and only the newest tip is
        kept -- else every op would be read once per copy.
        Sets RAD_COMMENT_METADATA_REF/_TYPE/_ID.  Returns 3 when the node holds no such issue or
        patch, 2 when the id matches more than one.

      rad_comment_metadata_assignees <storage> <ref>
        the item's current assignees
        The newest 'assign' op of the chain carries the whole set (a radicle op is the new value of
        its field), so the walk stops at the first one -- walking backwards from the tip is what makes
        that one read, not one read per op.  Only identities of the radicle system count here: an
        assignee which is not a DID/NID is dropped like any other entry without identity.

      rad_comment_metadata_collect <storage> <ref> <type> <id>
        the marker lines of one item
        Reads the chain of the one item the ref names -- never the whole repository: the ops and their
        payloads are opened for that COB alone ('rad_comment_metadata_ref' is what resolved which one
        it is).  Answers oldest first, and inside one second in chain order: the records are laid down
        in the order the chain is walked and the sort keeps that order (see below).
        Chronological, and inside one second in *chain order*: the records were laid down as the chain
        was walked (parent first), so a stable sort on the epoch keeps two markers written in the same
        second in the order they were written.  The op sha is deliberately *not* a tie break -- it
        would order a pair by hash, and '--last' would point at the wrong one of the two.

      rad_comment_metadata_newest <storage> <rid> <ref> <type> <id> <key>..
        the newest block per key
        The '--last' walk: newest op first, and it stops as soon as every requested key has its newest
        *trusted* entry -- so the common case opens one payload instead of the whole chain.  The walk
        skips a marker whose author is not attributable, and that is not a stopper: an older trusted
        block still applies, else one comment by a passer-by would freeze an item's state.
        'The newest block carrying that key, taken whole' is meant per key: every line of a key it
        found is returned (a comment proposing two labels is one application), and a key nobody asked
        for is left alone.  No <key> at all asks for *every* key, which is what the forward read
        answers; then the walk cannot stop early, because a key may first appear anywhere in the chain.
        Answers in the shape the forward read answers in -- oldest first, one
        '<epoch>US<iso>US<type>US<id>US<op>US<alias>US<did>US<trust>US<key>US<value>' per line.
        Walked in the order the forward read *ends* in -- newest epoch first, and inside one second the
        chain's last position -- so 'the newest block' is the last record of that answer and not merely
        the tip of the chain.  A chain whose dates are not monotone (an op replayed with an older
        clock) would otherwise let the two paths point at different records.
        The markers of this op.  A key nobody has answered yet is answered by this op -- with
        every line of the key it carries, so a comment proposing two labels is one application --
        and a key a *newer* op already answered is not answered again here.
        Oldest first, like the forward read: the chain position decides the order, never the date --
        an add and its undo land in the same second routinely (the walk above is newest first).

      rad_comment_metadata_json - the whole answer as one object
        Complete, never elided: the entries the filter kept, oldest first.
        The assignees belong into the answer: they are one of the reasons an entry is trusted, and a
        caller which sees a surprise wants to know where the trust came from.  Identities travel in
        the form the radicle tools want ('rad issue assign --add <DID>'), the same one the text
        output prints: a consumer must never have to re-normalize them.

      rad_comment_metadata_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr
        Which repository: an explicit one (RID, or a path entered to ask that checkout), else the one
        this runs in.  Sets 'RAD_METADATA_RID' and 'RAD_METADATA_STORAGE'.  '<name>' is the caller,
        only for its diagnostics -- 'not a repository here' and 'the node holds no storage' are not
        the same answer for the operator, and neither is a usage error.

      rad_comment_metadata see the module header
        the one item the question is about (the only positional argument there is)
        The trust specs are validated before anything is read: a usage error is not worth a node
        lookup, and it has to be reported the same way whether or not the node holds the repository.
        'rad' first: without it nothing can be resolved, and "the node holds no storage" would be
        the wrong answer for the operator to chase.
        Which repository: an explicit one (RID or path), else the checkout this runs in.
        Who is trusted: the delegates answer from the identity document, 'did:' names one identity and
        'contributor(<n>)' is decided from the authored commits the fact tool counts.  The table is
        module state, because the '--last' walk asks the same question from its own loop.
        An asked-for contributor threshold needs the facts command: without it every count reads
        'unknown', which would answer 'nothing is trusted' where the operator asked for trust.
        The item's assignees are a trust source of their own: assigning an issue to a contributor
        makes that contributor's proposals count -- so the same query can answer differently before
        and after an assignment.  That is the design: trust is read when it is asked for, never
        stored, and it is always an identity of the radicle system ('z6M…') which carries it.
        '--last' is the newest block *per key* and it is answered from the tip of the chain
        backwards: what applies is known without reading what it superseded.
        No radicle identity in the op's author (a git-only author, or a mail which is not
        '<alias>@<nid>'): there is nothing to authenticate against, so the proposal is dropped
        -- silently, like every other proposal which is not trusted.

      rad_metadata_rad <repo> <args>..
        one 'rad' invocation, in the repository the caller means
        Every applier below does exactly one invocation of 'rad' and this is where it runs: an empty
        <repo> is the checkout the command runs in, a path is entered, a RID is handed to rad with
        '-r' (rad then needs no checkout at all).  Nothing is written anywhere else and there is no
        second write: an applier either makes its one call or it refuses.

      rad_metadata_identity <repo> <did|alias>
        the DID the write commands take
        A comment cannot be trusted to spell a DID right, so an alias is resolved before it is written
        -- by the same fact command the trust gate asks ('RAD_TRUST_CMD', the tool behind 'rad_trust'),
        for the repository the caller names (or this checkout).  A value which is already a DID/NID
        needs no command; one which resolves to nothing is refused rather than written as it stands.

      rad_metadata_labels <repo> <type> <id>
        the labels the item carries now, one per line
        The mutation target, read fresh and never remembered: the Labels column of 'rad <type> list'
        is what the item carries, and the applier knows what it has to delete from it.  This is *not*
        how the chain state is read -- a label written after a proposal is stale -- it is a different
        question ('what is there' against 'what was proposed').
        '<type>:<id>' is the form 'rad_comment_metadata' answers with, so it is the form the appliers
        take; a bare id is an issue, which is the common case (labels and assignments are mostly issue
        business, and the read side names the type anyway).

      rad_set_label_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_set_label <item> [<add>] [<delete>].. [--repo <rid|path>]
        one 'rad <type> label'
        One label added, and any number of labels deleted in the same write: that is what makes an
        item carry one chain state at a time (the new one in, the previous one out).  '<add>' may be
        the empty word, which is no label at all -- a removal alone (a lone '*rmlabel:' clearing the
        chain slot with no successor) is the same write with nothing to add, and at least one of the
        two has to be given.  A label the item does not carry is rad's business, not this function's
        -- it hands rad exactly what it was given and reports what it did.

      rad_rm_label_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_rm_label <item> <glob>.. [--repo <rid|path>]
        remove the labels a glob matches
        The removal half of rad_set_label: what the item carries now is read fresh ('rad <type> list',
        the mutation target, never remembered) and every stored label matching one of the globs goes
        out in a single write.  A glob matches the whole stored label, anchored at both ends, so
        'pr:*' removes 'pr:<id>' and never 'xpr:<id>', and '*' removes every label -- the chain slot
        included, which is an explicit act.  A glob which matches nothing writes nothing and says so:
        nothing to remove is an answer, not a failure.  No identity is resolved -- a glob matches
        labels, never identities.

      rad_assign_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_assign <item> <did|alias> [--repo <rid|path>]
        one 'rad <type> assign --add'

      rad_unassign <item> <did|alias> [--repo <rid|path>]
        one 'rad <type> assign --delete'

      rad_label_is_protected <label>
        0 when the label is namespaced, hence protected
        The one rule of the label model, in one place: a label carrying a ':' is *namespaced* --
        'pr:<id>', 'issue:<id>' and every other '<namespace>:<rest>' -- and such a label is
        **protected**: the applier never deletes it implicitly, so it survives every chain
        transition, and it leaves the item only when a '*rmlabel:' glob or a direct write names it.
        A colon-free label is a candidate for the *chain slot*, and exactly one of those is kept per
        item: a new '*label:<colon-free>' replaces the stored one in the same write.
        The colon and nothing else decides this -- a hyphen or an underscore protects nothing, which
        is why 'needs-info' is a chain state like any other.  Values only: what matters is the stored
        label's own text, never where it came from.

      rad_apply_labels <repo> <dry> <entry>..
        the label state of one item, reconciled and applied
        The label entries of one item are one *state*: the labels the chain proposes against the
        labels the item carries, which are read fresh (nothing is remembered between calls).  Three
        rules are the whole model:
         * the **chain slot** is the colon-free label ('rad_label_is_protected').  Exactly one is
           kept, so a proposed colon-free label carries the stored one out in the same write, and a
           second one in the same block carries the first out in turn -- the block's last one wins,
           which is the 'a new *label:nopunct overwrites this' rule read within one block.
         * a **protected** label ('pr:<id>', 'issue:<id>') is never deleted implicitly.  It leaves the
           item only through a '*rmlabel:' or a direct write, so one the block does not name is
           reported and left alone.
         * a **proposal is what a write is made of**.  A block which names no colon-free label says
           nothing about the slot -- adding 'pr:<id>' is not a statement about the chain state -- and
           a block which carries no label at all writes nothing at all: what the item carries is
           reported instead.  Deleting on the strength of an absent proposal would reverse a
           delegate's write, and a delegate may label by hand with no comment at all; a missing line
           is ambiguous, never an instruction.
        An <entry> is one line of the reader's answer as 'key<TAB>item<TAB>id<TAB>value'; the entries
        which are not labels are not this function's business, except that they name the item.  A
        label the item already says is not written again, and the label form is 'rad_label_name's.
        What the chain proposes (every label of the item's block, in the write's own label form) and
        what the item carries now.
        No label proposed at all: report what the item carries, write nothing.
        A protected label the block does not name stays where it is, and says so.
        What to add, in the order the block wrote it -- and which of them is the chain slot.
        A block which names no colon-free label says nothing about the chain slot.
        Nothing to add and nothing to fix: what is left is the slot's own replacement, a removal
        alone, which the write layer takes as an add-less call.

      rad_apply_from_comments_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_apply_from_comments see the module header
        The maintenance side of the reader: it asks 'rad_comment_metadata' for the trusted proposals
        of an item ('--last', which is the state; a block a later one superseded is history) and
        dispatches every entry to the applier of its key.  The reader owns the trust decision -- an
        entry whose author cannot be attributed never reaches this function, and it is not reported
        here because it was never an entry -- and this function owns the effect.  The keys it knows
        are 'label', 'rmlabel', 'assign' and 'unassign'; any other key is reported and never guessed
        at.  The removals run first: a block may carry both a '*rmlabel:' and a '*label:' in the same
        namespace, and the add has to be the later act (otherwise the removal takes the new label out
        again).
        The removals first ('*rmlabel:'): a protected label ('pr:<id>', 'issue:<id>') leaves the
        item only this way, and a block carrying both a removal and an add in the same namespace
        has to end with the new label in -- which it does only when the removal ran before it.
        The labels then: they are one state of the item, and the write which changes it may have
        to delete the chain slot it replaces.  Then everything else in the order it was written.

      rad_reaction_accepts <char>
        0 when the store would accept it as a reaction
        One codepoint of rad's allow list (heartwood, 'crates/radicle/src/cob/common.rs').  An emoji
        picker hands out '❤️' (heart plus a variation selector) and other sequences, and the store
        refuses those with 'invalid reaction': refusing them here costs no call and no write, and it is
        the same list -- a character this accepts and the store does not would be a bad promise.
        U+1F600..U+1F64F, U+1FA75..U+1FA77, U+1FAC0..U+1FAC6, U+1FAE0..U+1FAF8,
        U+1F300..U+1F5FF, U+2700..U+27BF, U+1F900..U+1F9FF, U+1F680..U+1F6FF

      rad_metadata_reactions <storage> <ref> <kind> <target> <nid>
        our live markers on one target
        Reads the live reaction markers of *our* identity on one target, as 'rad cob show --format json'
        reduces them.
        <kind> names the part of the document to look at: 'comment' is an issue's thread (its root
        comment included), 'auto' every reaction-able part of a patch -- a revision, a comment of one, a
        review, a comment of a review.  <target> may be short: the answer carries the full id.
        The answer's first line names the target, '|'-separated --
        '<count>|<kind>|<id>|<review>|<revision>' -- and the lines after it are the live markers of
        ours, one per line.  Returns 3 when the document does not carry the target.

      rad_reaction_ref <storage> <id>
        the COB a reaction target names, in 'RAD_METADATA_REF'/_TYPE/_ID
        Resolves a patch, issue, revision, review or comment id to the COB which carries it.
        Returns 3 when nothing matches, 2 when the id matches more than one COB.

      rad_metadata_self - the NID of the identity 'rad' uses here

      rad_reaction_action <emoji> <active> <kind> <id> <review> <revision>
        one 'cob update' action
        The 'cob update' action for one marker on a target, as the payload rad's own commands write.

      rad_reaction_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_reaction see the module header
        Compare and swap on the caller's *own* reaction state: one target, one marker, and the write
        happens only when what the caller expects is what the store reduces.
        The '<from> <to>' form is the two leading positionals, the '<to> <target>' form starts with
        the reaction to set.  What tells them apart is what can be a reaction at all: a target is an
        id, never an emoji -- and an empty second word is only a '<to>' if that is what it can be,
        which is how 'rad_reaction <from> "" <target>' takes a marker back.
        The step changes exactly one marker, so '<from>' names exactly one: a value carrying several
        ('--from "✅ ❓"') is refused here -- it is no state a single step could both expect and resolve
        -- and the one marker is checked against the same allow list as '<to>' before any call.

      rad_topic_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_topic_json <root> <name> <path> <base> <head> <fetched>
        one object

      rad_topic see the module header
        Body of the 'rad_topic' rule.  Nothing is created before the class, the name, the clone,
        the base and the collisions have all been resolved, so a refusal leaves no half-made tree.
        The clone: always the *main* checkout, also when the caller sits inside a topic tree.
        The base: the clone's trunk -- upstream *plus* the local work, so branching off the remote
        tip would drop everything this host has not published yet.  The remote is fetched first to
        *know* the relation; a trunk which is behind upstream is refused, because updating the trunk
        is its own step with its own gate.

      rad_merge_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_merge_delegate <rid> <nid>
        0 when that identity is a delegate of the repository
        The delegate list is a fact about *today*: a repository can delegate us at any time and take
        it back, so it is read here, per run, never remembered.  A line is '<did> (<alias>)', so the
        identity is what stands before the first blank.

      rad_merge_ref <storage> <id>
        the COB ref of that patch, empty when it is not there
        A COB lives in the namespace of every identity which replicated it, so the newest copy is the
        one to read -- else every op would be read once per copy.

      rad_merge_read <storage> <ref> <rid>
        the head revision of a patch and its newest followup
        Sets the 'RAD_MERGE_*' facts of one patch:
          RAD_MERGE_REVISION       the head revision: the newest 'Create patch' or 'Add revision' op,
                                   an unrevised patch is its own head.  That op's own id is the id a
                                   review has to name -- 'rad patch list' shows the revision's commit
                                   instead, which is a different thing.
          RAD_MERGE_COMMIT         the commit that revision carries
          RAD_MERGE_ACCEPT         the identity which accepted it, empty when none did
          RAD_MERGE_STALE          why an acceptance found in the chain does not count, empty when
                                   none is stale: 'revision' (an older revision was accepted),
                                   'not-a-delegate', 'review:<verdict>' or 'followup:<kind>'
          RAD_MERGE_COMMENT        the newest comment of the chain, '<op> <nid>'
        The acceptance is the newest *followup* of the chain.  A reaction is not one: a marker is
        bookkeeping -- the reaction loop writes one on every event it reads -- so counting a marker
        as a followup would void the acceptance of every patch the moment somebody looks at it.
        Everything else is one: a comment, a new revision, an edit, a lifecycle op.  The walk is over
        the COB's own first parent chain (a COB commit carries the whole chain as parents), newest op
        first, and it stops at the head revision.

      rad_merge_target <toplevel> <patch>
        the branch that patch targets, empty when rad hides it
        'rad patch list' has no target column and the patch's own revision payload does not carry one
        either, so the header of 'rad patch show' is the one place which says it.

      rad_merge_upstream <toplevel> <target>
        the commit the canonical branch holds
        What a patch is merged into is the *canonical* branch as the last fetch left it.  Merging into
        the local branch instead would publish whatever else sits on it, which is not this helper's
        business.

      rad_merge_push <toplevel> <upstream> <head> <target> <patch>
        merge and push, print the commit
        'merge-tree' writes the merged tree without a working tree (and is the clean-merge test),
        'commit-tree' makes the merge commit, the push names that commit: no tree is ever switched, so
        a checkout somebody is working in stays exactly where it is.  A head the canonical branch
        already contains is pushed as it is -- a fast-forward, linear history, and the gate run at the
        head is the post-merge gate.
        Returns 1 when the push failed and 2 when it went somewhere else: 'refs/heads/<target>' is the
        canonical branch, and rad has to say that the canonical reference moved -- a push which
        succeeded without that line landed in our own namespace, which is not a merge.

      rad_merge_abort <toplevel> <storage> <revision> <upstream> <nid> <last-comment>
        the abort note
        'does not merge at upstream <sha>' is the comment the abort owes the author, and it goes on the
        revision it is about.  Once per head revision, not once per run: when our own newest comment
        already says exactly that, the patch was reported before and nothing is appended again.

      rad_merge_accepted_prs see the module header
        There is no '--repo': the checkout it is run from is the repository, and its canonical
        branch is the one the patches are merged into.
        Oldest first: an older topic has waited longest, and a merge into the same branch moves the
        base under every younger one anyway.
        Whether it merges at all is read in both modes -- that is the answer a dry run owes,
        and it writes nothing to find out.

      rad_cob_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_cob_call <args>..
        one 'rad' invocation, its answer (both streams) and its status
        Like 'rad_run', except that the answer is *returned* and nothing is reported: a store failure
        is itself an answer here -- the four failure cases are told apart by it, and the store's own
        first line is what 'refused' and 'absent' publish.  'rad' writes its error to stdout, so
        folding the streams in loses nothing.

      rad_cob_first_line <text>
        the first line of <text>

      rad_cob_fail <case> <type> <cob> <detail>
        the answer of a failure and its exit code
        One JSON line on stdout, so the four cases are told apart by what a consumer already reads.
        'detail' is the store's own first line where there is one: a paraphrase would hide the
        sentence the operator has to search for.

      rad_cob_typename <type>
        the store's type name, 1 when it is not one of the three

      rad_cob_repo <verb> <repo>
        the repository a read is about, in RAD_COB_*
        A RID is answered by the node alone ('rad' then needs no checkout); a path -- or nothing,
        which is the checkout the command runs in -- is named by 'rad inspect --rid'.  A RID the node
        holds no storage for is 'notfound' (the repository is not here), while a node which cannot
        even say where its storage is has nothing to read at all ('unreadable').
        No storage for that RID is 'not found' only when the node itself answers: a profile
        which is not there, or a node which cannot be reached, has nothing to read at all, and a
        consumer must not read that as 'the repository is gone'.

      rad_cob_ids <rid> <typename>
        the ids the store holds of one type, one per line

      rad_cob_tip <ops-json>
        the id of the newest op rad printed, empty when there is none

      rad_cob_copy_note <storage> <type> <id> <op_id>
        name the copy a read landed on
        Sets 'RAD_COB_COPY' to the namespace a read landed on, and says it on stderr -- always, not
        at some verbosity: it is the answer to 'which copy is this', and a consumer that derives a
        verdict from a chain behind the newest copy has to be able to see that.  The op's id is the
        commit a namespace ref points at, so the tip names its copies; when the newest copy is a
        different tip the answer is behind it, and that is a loud line, not a note.

      rad_cob_list see the module header

      rad_cob_ops see the module header
        The store's own listing decides 'not found' from 'absent': an id it lists whose chain does
        not materialise is absent, an id it does not list does not exist.  Without the listing the
        two are the same message, and a consumer would act on 'gone' for both.
        'rad cob log' prints newest first; a chain is read oldest first.  '--from' and '--until'
        narrow the same chain and both bounds are inclusive (measured on rad 1.10.3), so the window
        is reversed rather than re-derived.

      rad_cob_document see the module header
        One offset for the whole document, taken once: the store's timestamps carry no zone, and one
        call per timestamp would cost a process each.  '$zone' is what 'date +%z' prints, so a date
        here reads like 'date --iso-8601=seconds' renders it.

      rad_cob see the module header
        Every verb's answer is a JSON object -- including the one that names a failure -- so jq is a
        dependency of the family, checked once, before a verb spends a call on the store.

      rad_rebase_ask_usage [reason]
        usage on stderr

      rad_rebase_show_field <show> <field>
        one header value of 'rad patch show'
        '<show>' is the whole output.  The header line reads '│ <Field>   <value>' and the value is
        padded to the box, so it is cut exactly like a table row: the field name is the cell 'rad_line'
        drops, and what is left is the value.  Never parsed from the document -- a patch's own
        revision payload carries no branch, and the COB's 'target' field is 'delegates' (the *review*
        target, who is asked to review), not the branch.  'rad patch list' has no such column either.

      rad_rebase_live_comment <storage> <ref>
        the newest comment of the chain, and its body
        Sets 'RAD_REBASE_COMMENT' (the op id) and 'RAD_REBASE_BODY'.  Empty when the chain has no
        comment at all.
        Redaction-aware, and it has to be: a COB chain is append-only, so a directive the loop has
        already consumed is still in the history -- its deletion is a 'Redact comment' op naming it --
        and a read that trusted the text would call a spent '*trigger:rebase' live and never ask that
        patch again.  Walked newest first, so a redaction is always read before the comment it removes.

      rad_rebase_ask see the module header
        The branch: what the merge landed on, never a guess.  '--after' asks the merged patch itself
        (rad resolves its upstream, else the repository's default branch), '--branch' names it
        outright, and with neither it is the default branch the node's storage points at -- a
        delegate may make any branch the default, so the name decides nothing and is not inspected.
        A merge that did not land is not a merge.  The patch has to read 'merged' (above) *and* the
        commit it was merged as has to be in the branch now: a push that failed, a merge into another
        branch, or a landing that invented its own base is not something the siblings are asked about.
        The patch that just landed is no member: it is closed by the merge, and were it still
        open its own head is the thing that landed -- asking it to re-cut would move what the
        branch now holds.  Compared as a prefix, because the two sides are read from different
        places: the listing prints seven hex, '--after' is the full id the caller was handed.
        The write needs the full 40-hex id: 'rad patch list' prints seven and 'rad patch
        comment' resolves only the whole object id, so a directive posted under the listing's id
        is refused for every member -- measured after one landing, 30 refusals and not one
        directive.  The header carries it, so it costs no extra call.
        Another author's patch is not ours to publish: a revision only ever goes into the
        namespace of its own author, so a directive there could not be worked.
        A directive already standing is never sent twice: the sole line is always the
        same text, so a second one is a duplicate op, and the loop answers it as such.
        The one thing that may not be guessed at: a write under a short id is refused,
        and the refusal reads like a repository refusing us rather than a wrong id form.

    Rules:

      rad_import
        Imports the repository at the toplevel.  Not a question but the way in: it changes the repository
        (a new remote, the branches pushed) and says what it did.

      rad_state
        The radicle state of this checkout: repository, identity, patches, issues and the branches
        which still carry local work.

      rad_patches
        The patches of this checkout, by state and by author.

      rad_trust
        The trust verdict of the triage policy; exit 0 trusted, 1 untrusted, 2 could not answer.

      rad_comment_metadata
        Who is trusted is read when the question is asked, from the repository (its delegates), from the
        item (the identities it is assigned to) and from the command line ('--trust'); 'contributor(<n>)'
        asks 'RAD_TRUST_CMD' for the commits that identity authored as itself -- the gate resolves into
        radicle identities, commits of plain git authors are not counted.  Only identities of the radicle
        system carry trust -- an op whose author or assignee is not a DID/NID is dropped, silently, and
        'RAD_TRUST_CMD' being unset is only an error when a contributor threshold was asked for.  A
        proposal which is not trusted yet can become trusted later (a delegate assigns the item to its
        author), so the answer to the same query is allowed to change between runs.

      rad_topic
        Creates '<clone>/.worktrees/<class>-<description>' on the branch of the same name; exit 0 created,
        1 refused (the tree or the branch exists, the name is too long), 2 could not answer (usage, an
        unknown class, not a git repository, git failed).

      rad_reaction
        Compare and swap on the caller's *own* reaction state: a reaction is private per identity and can
        never be delegated, so there is no trust question here.  '<to>' is the marker to set (one codepoint
        of the store's allow list, checked before any call), '<from>' what the caller expects to be live
        there -- omitted is no expectation at all, the empty string is 'no live marker of mine'.  An empty
        '<to>' withdraws '<from>' and sets nothing: patch level only, and it needs '--from', because the
        expectation is what names the marker.  A target is '<issue-id>', '<issue-id> <comment-id>',
        '<patch-or-revision-id>' (a patch id is its root revision) or '<patch-or-revision-id> <comment-id>'.
        Exit 0 applied, 1 the expected marker is not what the store reduces (nothing was written),
        2 could not answer (usage, a reaction the store would not accept, a missing target),
        3 the node holds no such repository, item or revision.

      rad_cob
        Exit 0 answered, 1 the store refused (its own first line is 'detail', unchanged), 2 usage, 3 no
        such repository, type or object, 4 the store lists the object and nothing materialises, 5 the
        store or the node cannot be read.  Each failure is one JSON line on stdout, so a consumer which
        reads the exit code and stdout alone tells them apart.

      rad_merge_accepted_prs
        The acceptance is read off the patch's own chain -- a delegate's 'review' with 'verdict: accept'
        naming the head revision, with nothing but reactions after it, else it has to be accepted again --
        and the merge is a push into the canonical branch, which only a delegate may make: an invocation
        under any other identity refuses before it writes.  '--dry-run' answers what is ready and writes
        nothing.  Exit 0 answered, 1 refused or could not answer, 2 usage, 3 no data.

      rad_rebase_ask
        A *sender*, nothing more: it merges nothing, re-cuts nothing and opens no worktree.  It posts one
        comment -- the sole line '*trigger:rebase' -- on every pending patch of ours whose branch has just
        moved, so the re-cut materializes in that patch's own round (its own claim, worktree, session and
        gate).  '--after <patch>' is the merge path: the patch has to read 'Status merged' and the commit
        it was merged as has to be in the branch now, else nothing is asked and the exit code says so --
        a failed or unreadable merge is never followed by a sweep.  The branch is the merged patch's
        'Target' (rad resolves the upstream, else the repository's default branch, whatever it is named);
        '--branch' names one outright when there is no merge to point at, and '--onto' overrides the
        branch tip the members are measured against.  A patch is skipped when its 'Target' is another
        branch, when it is another author's (a revision only goes into the author's own namespace), when
        its head already contains the tip, and when a live '*trigger:rebase' already stands -- a consumed
        one is redacted in the chain and does not count.  Exit 0 nothing failed, 1 a refusal or a failed
        write, 2 usage, 3 no data.

      rad_rm_label
        The removal half of the write layer's label effect: the labels the item carries are read fresh
        ('rad <type> list', the mutation target, never remembered) and every one matching a glob goes out
        in a single 'rad <type> label --delete' invocation.  A glob matches the whole stored label,
        anchored at both ends, with '*' and '?': 'pr:*' removes 'pr:<id>' and never 'xpr:<id>', and '*'
        removes every label -- the chain slot included, an explicit act.  A glob matching nothing writes
        nothing and is reported: nothing to remove is an answer, not a failure.  No identity is resolved,
        a glob matches labels and never identities.  Exit 0 removed (or nothing matched), 1 the write 'rad'
        refused or could not answer, 2 usage.

  rad_rules - The rules of the 'rad' module which everything else may depend on before a rulefile is read:
    the cheap guards of a radicle checkout and the hook into 'activate'.
    
    'bar' requires every '*_rules' file on every invocation, so what lives here is a predicate or a
    hook and nothing else: the heavy bodies, the tables and the rendering are in 'Bar.d/rad' and
    'Bar.d/radicle_lib', which are loaded when a 'rad_*' rule actually runs.  A '_rules' file which
    carries neither a clause nor a guard is dead weight parsed on every run.
    
      is_rad_available   the command bar runs ('RAD_BIN', else 'rad') is here at all
      is_rad_repository  that command answers for this checkout ('rad inspect --rid')
      is_rad_toplevel    we are at the toplevel of such a repository
      rad_activate       keep '.worktrees/' out of 'git status' (hooked into 'activate')
    
    The predicates answer about the same command the helpers run -- 'RAD_BIN' when it is set, 'rad'
    otherwise -- without pulling the library in for it.  A predicate answers *itself*: it is 0 or 1
    and a missing command is a 1, not a skip.  A rule which only wants the gate writes it with a
    question mark ('is_rad_repository? something') and is skipped when it does not hold.
    
    The predicates are deliberately *not* registered as repertoire helpers: a helper answers a
    retrieval question with an output, a predicate prints nothing and is only ever the dependency of
    another rule ('Bar.d/helpers_rules' documents that split).
    
    CONSIDERED AND NOT WIRED -- nothing consumes them yet; each becomes a clause here when something
    does:
    
      'rad sync --announce' in a publish hook -- measured on this host: every 'git push rad' and
        every 'rad patch edit' syncs with the seeds by itself ('Synced with 3 seed(s)') and a push
        announces the repository ('rad ls' flips 'local' to 'public'), so such a hook would only
        repeat what the push already did and pay a network round trip for it;
      a 'fetch_resources' clause ('git fetch rad') -- it would put the network on a path which
        'lints' runs through on every invocation;
      an 'agent' clause failing when 'a local branch carries commits no patch exposes' -- true for
        every commit in progress, far too noisy for the check an agent runs after each change.

    Rules:

      is_rad_available
        Succeeds when the command bar runs is available: 'RAD_BIN' when it is set, else 'rad'.

      is_rad_repository
        Succeeds when that command answers for this checkout, that is when it is a radicle repository.
        A missing command is a *no* here, not a skip and not the 127 of a shell that cannot find it.

      is_rad_toplevel
        Succeeds when we are at the toplevel of a radicle repository.

      rad_activate
        Maintain a radicle checkout for bar: keep '.worktrees/' -- where 'rad_topic' puts the topic trees
        -- out of 'git status', through '.git/info/exclude'.  Setup, not a question: it writes the line
        once and is silent when it is already there.

  radicle_lib - Shared radicle plumbing: run 'rad', read what it prints, and emit the JSON literals.
    
    The radicle domains of bar -- the state of a checkout, the flow around an incoming item, the way
    a repository gets onto radicle (all of them 'rad_*' rules of Bar.d/rad) -- ask the same small
    questions about the tool and its data.  The answers live here once, named after the command they
    belong to:
    
      rad_run                run '$RAD_BIN' and carry its answer and its exit code
      rad_path               where the node keeps its storage ('rad path')
      rad_cobs_time          when and how the last event of a patch or issue happened, read from
                             'refs/namespaces/*/refs/cobs/xyz.radicle.<type>' below that storage
      rad_cob_copies         every namespace copy of one COB, newest first ('<date> <tip> <nid>')
      rad_cob_newest         the newest of those, and 'rad_cob_nids' the namespaces carrying a tip
      rad_age                an epoch as a short age ('6m', '2h', '3d', '1y')
      rad_line/rad_columns/rad_table
                             cut the padded, boxed tables 'rad' prints by the names of their
                             columns
      rad_exclude_worktrees  keep '<clone>/.worktrees/' out of 'git status'
    
    The JSON literals of the '--json' forms are not rad's business: every module of bar which emits
    one asks Bar.d/json_lib, which this library requires ('json_string', 'json_number', 'json_bool',
    'json_list').
    
    This module defines no rules.  It is the backing library of the 'rad' word, required by the
    module which carries those rules ('Bar.d/rad') -- a rule name like 'rad_state' makes
    'rule_autoload' look for a module 'rad_lib', which this file is not: the library of the radicle
    domain is named after the protocol, not after the word.  Nothing here goes to the network and
    nothing guesses: a function which cannot answer returns non-zero ('rad_run' tells 'no radicle
    repository here' (3) from 'rad failed' (1), see there), and the callers own their diagnostics.
    
    'RAD_BIN' names the command to run instead of 'rad' (the tests point it at a fake).
    'RAD_US' is the field separator of the records these functions return and take
    ('rad_table' hands its cells over that way, US for the same reason Bar.d/helpers_rules uses it:
    a field may be empty and a space would collapse it), and 'RAD_RS' is the field separator
    of a 'git for-each-ref' line.  Reading rad's tables back needs a character aware shell; bar
    exports a UTF-8 'LC_ALL' whenever the system has one.

    Variables:

      RAD_BIN (gx)
        the command to use instead of 'rad'
        Default: ${RAD_BIN:-rad}

      RAD_US (gr)
        the field separator of the records 'rad_table' returns
        Default: $'\037'

      RAD_RS (gr)
        the field separator of a 'git for-each-ref' line
        Default: $'\011'

    Functions:

      rad_run <args>..
        run '$RAD_BIN' and print what it wrote
        Fails with 1 and a diagnostic on stderr when rad failed, with 3 when the failure was that
        there is no radicle repository: stderr is folded into the output so both can be told apart.

      rad_path - the radicle home of this node, empty when 'rad path' does not answer
        Memoized: the COB reader asks for it once per object type, and every caller within one run
        wants the same answer.

      rad_cobs_time <type> <rid> <array>
        when and how the last event of every <type> happened
        The kind and the time of the last event of every patch and issue into the associative
        array <array> named by the caller, as '<type>:<id>' -> '<updated> <kind>'.  Every namespace
        of the node storage keeps a copy of a COB, so the newest commit over all of them is the
        last event and its subject names its kind ('Comment', 'Merge revision', ...).  A storage
        which is not there is not an error, the times stay empty then.

      rad_cob_copies <storage> <type> <id>
        every namespace copy of one COB, newest first
        Every namespace of the node storage keeps its own copy of a COB, and a peer's copy can be
        ahead of ours.  One '<date> <tip> <nid>' line per copy, in one stable order -- the commit
        date (unix) descending, the namespace ascending to break a tie -- so 'the newest' is a
        decision a caller reads off the first line and never a wildcard match.  A storage which
        cannot be read, or which carries no copy, answers nothing and succeeds.

      rad_cob_newest <storage> <type> <id>
        the newest copy of one COB, '<date> <tip> <nid>'
        The one line 'rad_cob_copies' put first, and nothing when there is no copy.  Read into an
        array rather than piped through 'head': a consumer of a pipe that closes early fails under
        'pipefail' in the caller, and this answer is not worth such a surprise.

      rad_cob_nids <storage> <type> <id> <tip>
        the namespaces whose copy carries <tip>
        Which copy an op chain came from: the op's id *is* the commit the namespace ref points at,
        so the namespaces sharing that tip are the copies a read of it could have landed on.  One
        nid per word, with a trailing space; empty when no copy carries that tip.

      rad_age <now> <epoch>
        a short age ('6m', '2h', '3d', '1y'), '-' when there is no epoch

      rad_line <line>
        a table line without the box edges, the state marker cell and the padding
        '│ ●  a1bd13a  title  ...  │' -> 'a1bd13a  title  ...'.  The marker cell is a single token
        ('●' or '✓', whatever rad prints there) and is dropped: the columns behind it then start at
        the same offset in the header line and in every row, which is what 'rad_table' cuts by.

      rad_columns <header>
        the start offset of every column of a normalized table header
        A column starts where a run of two or more spaces ends and content follows (plus the first
        one): the two spaces are the separator between two cells, the padding of a cell is never
        part of its neighbour.  Neither a name nor a value is needed for this, the header line
        carries the layout itself.  The offsets are space separated.

      rad_table <table> <column>..
        the named cells of every row of a 'rad' table, US separated
        The columns are located by name in the header line (see 'rad_columns' for where a column
        starts), then every row is cut at the same offsets -- up to where the next column begins,
        so that a requested cell never carries the ones behind it.  Cut and not split: a cell may
        itself contain two spaces (a truncated author reads '<author>  (you)'), which splitting on
        whitespace would tear apart.  Every requested name has to exist in the table, otherwise
        this returns 1 and the caller reports that rad's output could not be understood.

      rad_exclude_worktrees <clone>
        keep '<clone>/.worktrees/' out of 'git status'
        The line goes into '.git/info/exclude', never into a tracked '.gitignore': the main checkout
        has to stay byte-identical, and a tree which exists is not a fact about the project.

  release - Rules for software releases
    
    The 'release' module implements a (opinionated) release workflow. Making releases starts
    on a git toplevel in a development or main branch, a devel branch does not need to be
    clean.  For each release a new branch/worktree based on the last commit is created. All
    release work will be done in that branch/worktree. This can be a interactive process, when
    release_tests fails one can correct things and commit them until the tests pass. The
    actual release procedure then evaluates 'release_prepare', 'release_commit', 'release_tag'
    and 'release_publish'.  Finally 'release_postprocess' and 'release_cleanup' are evaluated.
    
    Any non published release can be aborted/deleted.
    
    This only defines the bare workflow the actual implementation hooks into the
    'release_rules'.
    
    We still make a few fixed assumptions:
     - Versioning is based on semver
     - Releases are called 'release'
     - The main branch can be named 'main' or 'master'
     - Development branches matching 'devel' 'devel-*' or 'devel/*'
    
    The worktree tracks its state in a local git config bar.release.state:
     - start:
       The worktree is created but not tested yet.
     - tested:
       All test passed.
     - changelog:
       generated the changelog and committed it.
     - ready:
       The worktree/branch is ready for publishing
     - published:
       uploads succeeded.
     - done:
       The release finished.
    
    Further following git config variables are set:
     - bar.release.startbranch:
       Branch this release initiated from (usually main or devel).
     - bar.release.version:
       The (semver) version of this release.
    
    Release Configuration and Workflow
    
    Every release starts in its own git worktree. All testing, and fixes necessary for the
    release are made in that worktree.
    
    Configuration of release workflows is done by extending the rules from 'release_rules'.
    Many other '*_rules' modules already hook into these rules and do the 'right thing'.
    The parts that can configured in Barf are:
      - release_prepare:
        Any extra preparation for releases. 'cargo_rules' already bumps the version.
      - release_tests:
        Whatever tests are necessary for making a software release. 'lints' and 'tests' are
        already defined dependencies. Anything more needs to be added in Barf.
      - release_preprocess:
        Any final work that needs to be done before publishing. This should not change the
        project files anymore. This is the place to do git tags, merge/fast-forward branches
        or switch over to other worktrees. For convenience the 'release_as_*' rules exist to
        do this work.
      - release_publish:
        The actual publishing part. May need access to the internet.
        The 'git_rules' and 'cargo_rules' already hook into this.
      - release_postprocess:
        Any work to be done after the publishing. Like rebase work/devel branches on the
        release and so on.
      - release_cleanup:

    Functions:

      release_state_matches [[!]statematch]
        Check if the current state matches (does not match with '!').

      release_into <target_version>
        switch over to another existing release worktree, reset it to the current release state
        We prepare releases in dedicated worktrees. This would leave a lot worktrees behind.
        This provides the facility to coalesce worktrees to keep only major, majorpre1x, or majorminor versions.

      release_worktree_rename <version>
        rename worktree and cd into that

    Rules:

      release
        Makes a software release. Takes an optional version number starting with a digit or a
        name for the generating the version by 'release_generate_version_hook' as argument.
        When no argument is provided it defaults to 'auto'.

      release_as_major
        To be used from release-preprocess. Creates or resets the release worktree to release.x major.

      release_as_majorpre1x
        To be used from release-preprocess. Creates or resets the release worktree to release.x[.y] major with 0.x major semantic.

      release_as_majorminor
        To be used from release-preprocess. Creates or resets the release worktree to release.x.y major.minor.

  release_rules - The rules called by the release rule

    Rules:

      release_prepare
        Update manifest, bump versions, generate changelog an other versioned artifacts etc..
        Should leave the tree in a uncommited state. Note that 'build_versioned_artifacts' is
        already a dependency here.

      release_tests
        All tests and lints to run for a software release.

      release_changelog
        Create the changelog and commit it, may include tags etc.

      release_preprocess
        Local actions to be done before the release, rebase main/devel on it etc.

      release_publish
        Deploy the release, upload it to servers, git push or whatever necessary. Since network
        operations can be fallible this can be restarted.  Should start with them least critical
        (git push) and finally publish it to registries.

      release_postprocess
        Local actions to be done after the release.

      release_cleanup
        Final cleanup for the release, clean/delete worktrees etc

      release_generate_version
        Canonicalize version names. Queried when the the version to 'release' does not start with a
        digit or was not given. Other modules should plug in here. Names are 'auto', 'major',
        'minor', 'patch'. Custom rules may support more names such as 'prerelease', 'alpha', 'beta'
        and so on. 'auto' is the default for 'release'.

      release_current_version
        Query the current version of this project.

  rule_lib - The library that implements rules, always loaded

    Variables:

      BAR_KEEP_GOING (gx)
        Controls keep-going mode: 0 or 1 stops at first failure (default), numbers >= 2 allow that many failures before stopping, non-numeric values (yes/true/etc) continue until exhaustion. In keep-going mode, pure clauses continue executing after failures though failures are still propagated. Non-pure or conclusive clauses halt keep-going within the current rule.
        Default: ${BAR_KEEP_GOING:-1}

    Functions:
        See RULE DEFINITION SYNTAX in help for details

      rule_install_dep <name>
        makes what a clause dependency names reachable
        Called by :func:`rule_install_deps` while BAR_INSTALL_DEPS is set.  A dependency is
        resolved the way the evaluator resolves it, only without failing: this runs at *definition*
        time, where the rule that names the dependency is not evaluated at all, so a dependency
        nothing provides must not stop the walk (it is a dependency of a rule this project never
        calls, or one of the optional ones).  Loading the module a dependency lives in may define
        further rules, which walk their own dependencies in turn.

      rule_install_deps <body> [deps..]
        loads what a clause's body and dependencies need
        Called from :func:`rule` while BAR_INSTALL_DEPS is set.  Every dependency is made
        reachable (and the module it lives in loaded, transitively) and the body is walked line by
        line: a line which is a 'require' command is executed.  Nothing is evaluated, this only
        makes sure the rules, commands and modules a clause names exist.
        A dependency with a '??' suffix is one the evaluator only runs *when it exists* -- forcing
        it here would install modules a project never uses, so it is skipped.

      newrule <name:> ...
        Creates a new rule, deletes the existing one.
        Used to replace already existing rules/clauses.

      rule_delete <name>
        Deletes a rule (and all it clauses).
        Can only be used when the rule was not evaluated yet.

      rule_rename <oldname> <newname>
        renames a rule
        Can only be used when the rule was not evaluated yet.

      clause_local [var[=value]..]
        Makes scalar variables local to a clause.

      rule_eval <name> [args..]
        Evaluates a rule
        Normally this is used by bar itself but it can be used from function and rule bodies
        to evaluate rules.

      rule_exists <name>
        checks if a rule is defined

      rule_list [pattern]
        lists all loaded rules or rules matching pattern
        Because of automatic module loading this list is only a current view for diagnostics.

  run - Module for running tests in resource limited environments

    Variables:

      NICE_LEVEL (gx)
        The nice level 'run_test' uses
        Default: ${NICE_LEVEL:-1}

      TIMEOUT_SECS (gx)
        Timeout for tests
        Default: ${TIMEOUT_SECS:-300}

      MEMORY_KB (gx)
        Memory limit for tests
        Default: ${MEMORY_KB:-33554432}

  search - Rules to search text in a project

    Rules:

      search_for [rg_args..]
        General searching for text, pretty paging the results.

      search_issues
        Searches for 'FIXME:|TODO:|PLANNED:' with some context around.

      search_fixmes
        Searches for 'FIXME:'.

      search_todos
        Searches for 'TODO:'.

      search_planned
        Searches 'PLANNED:'.

  semver_lib - Parsing and manipulating (simplified) semantic versioning strings
    
    For the sake of sanity not all possible semver syntax variants are
    handled. Esp. manipulation is only implemented for the common cases. Patches welcome.

    Functions:

      semver_parse <semver> [major [minor [patch [prerelease [build]]]]]
        parses semver into the provided variables

      semver_validate <version>
        Validate if string is a valid semver

      semver_shortversion <version>
        shortens a semver to the first non zero part 0.2.1-pre0+123 -> 0.2

      semver_major <version>
        shortens a semver to major

      semver_majorpre1x <version>
        shortens a semver to major with 0.x major semantic

      semver_majorminor <version>
        shortens a semver to major.minor

      semver_is_patch <version>

      semver_increment <version> <release|major|majorpre1x|minor|patch>
        Increment selected part
        'release' strips any 'prerelease' part
        'majorpre1x' increments the minor part on a 0.x version

      semver_cmp <a> <b>
        comparing 'a' with 'b', prints 'lt', 'eq' or 'gt'

      semver_lt <a> <b>
        succeeds if a < b

      semver_le <a> <b>
        succeeds if a <= b

      semver_gt <a> <b>
        succeeds if a > b

      semver_ge <a> <b>
        succeeds if a >= b

      semver_eq <a> <b>
        succeeds if a == b

  shellcheck - Support for the shellcheck shell linter
    
    Shellcheck is surprisingly slow.  In projects using bar one can prevent the bar files
    themselves being checked by setting in 'Barf':
    
     SHELLCHECK_LS_FILES=":!bar :!Bar.d"  # Perhaps adding ':!Barf' too

    Variables:

      SHELLCHECK_LS_FILES (gx)
        List of file match rules for git-ls-files.
        Default: ${SHELLCHECK_LS_FILES:-}

    Functions:

      shellcheck_is_shscript <file>
        Check if <file> is a shell script

      shellcheck_list_shscripts List all shell scripts under git control

      shellcheck_lint Test if all shell scripts under git control pass the linter.
        '--color' (without a value) forces ANSI colour even when the output is not a terminal, so
        every captured log or memodb entry was full of escape sequences.  '--color=auto' is the
        shellcheck default: colour for a human at a terminal, plain text for a capture.

    Rules:

      shellcheck_has_shscripts
        Checks if the project has any shell scripts. Must be called from a git toplevel.
        Checks whenever any shell scripts are versioned in gitq

  shellcheck_rules - Hooks shellcheck_lint into the std lint rule

    Rules:

      is_shellcheck_available
        Checks if shellcheck is installed and the project has any shell scripts.

  sourcetree - The source trees below a source-work root: the clones, their worktrees, their state and what
    is not merged yet.
    
    A source-work root holds one clone per project and every topic lives in a *worktree* below
    '<clone>/.worktrees/<topic>'.  The main checkout of a clone is the local trunk and the single
    place where topics meet -- a topic branch is branched off it and merged back into it, and the
    main checkout is the only coordination point.  A topic branch that exists nowhere else is never
    reclaimed, so 'what is not merged yet' is the question that has to be answered before anything
    is cleaned up.
    
    Answering it the obvious way costs a 'git worktree list', a 'git status' per tree, a merge base
    question per tree and a shell loop to line the results up -- a dozen commands and a page of
    output for a handful of facts.  This helper answers it in one line per tree.
    
      bar sourcetree_state                 # the digest of '$BAR_SOURCE_ROOT'
      bar sourcetree_state <root>          # the digest of the source-work root named here
      bar sourcetree_state --json <root>   # the same digest as one JSON object, for a wrapper
    
    THE ROOT
    
    <root> is the source-work root: the argument, or '$BAR_SOURCE_ROOT' when the argument is not
    given.  There is no default root -- with neither, the helper cannot know which layout is meant
    and answers a usage error.  A root which is itself a repository (a '.git' at its top, or a bare
    git directory) is reported as that one clone -- handy for a repository which is not part of a
    source-work root -- otherwise every direct sub-directory holding a repository is one.  The walk
    is one level deep and never descends into a clone or into object, build or vendor directories:
    a clone belonging to a clone is not part of the layout this helper describes.
    
    A root which is not a source-work root -- a flat index of every checkout ever made, variants of
    one project side by side, no worktrees and no current state -- is named on stderr: a clone in
    such a root is history, not the working tree a topic is built on.  The answer is still printed,
    because auditing exactly that may be the intent.
    
    THE TREES
    
    One line per tree -- the main checkout of a clone first, then the clone's own worktrees and
    last the trees it registered elsewhere, each group by path -- followed by the total counts:
    the order only depends on the layout, so two digests of the same layout are comparable line
    for line and a new tree does not reshuffle the rest.
    
      <path>  <kind>  <branch>@<head>  <state>  <relation>  [<flags>]
    
      <path>      the tree, relative to <root> -- '.' for the root itself -- or absolute when the
                  tree lives outside of the root
      <kind>      'main'  the clone's trunk, the merge base of the clone
                  'worktree'  a worktree below '<clone>/.worktrees/'
                  'external'  a worktree registered elsewhere; Hermes' worktree GC never
                              reclaims an externally registered tree
                  'bare'  a bare repository (no working tree), 'broken'  no usable git
      <branch>    the checked out branch -- '(detached)' or '(unborn)' when it is not a branch --
                  and '@<head>', the short commit of that tree ('-' while it has none)
      <state>     'clean'  nothing to report
                  'dirty <changed>/<untracked>'  files git reports as modified, staged, renamed
                        or untracked; the two counts are kept apart because a tree full of build
                        output is untracked while a tree with a touched tracked file is changed
                  '?'  git refused to answer, '-'  bare
      <relation>  for the main checkout: 'wtexclude <sources>' -- how '.worktrees/' is kept out
                  of the checkout: 'local' ('.git/info/exclude', the convention), 'gitignore'
                  (a *tracked* '.gitignore': the machinery put it there on first use, it is
                  project pollution and the usual reason a main checkout reports as dirty) or
                  'none'.  Sources are joined with '+' when both are present.
      <relation>  for a worktree: 'unmerged <n> behind <m>' -- '<n>' commits of that tree are not
                  reachable from the main checkout (exactly what a merge would bring in, the two
                  halves of the merge base) and '<m>' commits of the main checkout are not in that
                  tree; 'unmerged 0 behind 0' means 'in step with the trunk'
      <flags>     'locked' (a stale worktree lock, with its reason), 'prunable'
    
    The last line is the total: '(N clones, M worktrees, U unmerged, D dirty)'.
    
    '--json' prints one object with those counts, the resolved 'root' and 'trees', one object per
    tree: 'path' (as printed), 'abs', 'clone', 'clone_abs', 'kind', 'branch', 'commit', 'head',
    'detached', 'state', 'changed', 'untracked', 'unmerged', 'behind', 'merge_base', 'locked',
    'lock_reason', 'prunable', 'bare' and 'exclude' (the sources of the '.worktrees/' exclusion).
    A number git could not answer is 'null'.  Text for reading, the object for a wrapper; both are
    the same single pass over the same data.
    
    EXIT CODES
    
      0  answered -- a root without any clone answers '(0 clones, 0 worktrees, 0 unmerged, 0 dirty)'
      2  usage error, the usage line goes to stderr and stdout stays empty
      3  <root> is not a directory: the question could not be asked
    
    COST (measured on this host, 2026-10-10)
    
    A root with four clones and thirteen worktrees -- 18 lines, 2.2 kB of text or 8.3 kB of JSON --
    takes 0.3 s (best of five; a bare 'bar rule_exists' call takes 0.09 s), the digest of a
    throw-away root with one clone and three trees 0.15 s.  Two git calls per tree are the
    cost ('status' and the left-right count, plus 'merge-base' when a tree is both ahead and
    behind); the layout is read through them once, nothing is paged and no interface is entered
    which refuses to run outside its own context.
    
    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
    
    It reports, it never repairs: no fetch, no prune, no removal of a worktree, no edit of a
    '.gitignore' and no judgement about whether a topic should live on.  Diffing two digests is
    how a change (a merge, a new topic, a dirtied trunk) is seen.

    Variables:

      SOURCETREE_ROW (gA)
        The row 'sourcetree_record' stores, filled by the collector.
        Default: ()

      SOURCETREE_ROWS (ga)
        The rows of the clone being collected, sorted before printing.
        Default: ()

    Functions:

      sourcetree_json_flag <var> <flags> <name>
        put 'true' into <var> when the flag is set

      sourcetree_exclude_json <var> <text>
        put the exclusion sources into <var> as a JSON array
        The sources ('local', 'gitignore') arrive joined by '+'; 'none' is no source, which is the
        empty array and not the array of the word -- that is sourcetree's policy about its own field,
        the array literal is 'json_list'.

      sourcetree_relative <var> <root> <path>
        put <path> as printed, relative to <root>, into <var>
        A tree outside the root keeps its absolute path (it has no name below the root); the root
        itself is '.', so a root which is a repository names its own main checkout.

      sourcetree_pitfall <root>
        name a root which is not a source-work root on stderr
        A source-work root holds one clone per project and every topic in a worktree below
        '<clone>/.worktrees/'.  A flat index of every checkout ever made -- variants of one project
        side by side, no worktrees, and no current state -- has the clones but none of the
        worktrees; inventorying it as if it were the working tree a topic is built on is the
        mistake this helper exists to prevent, so such a root is named when it happens.

      sourcetree_is_repo <dir>
        it is a git repository: a '.git' entry or a bare git directory
        A bare repository has no '.git' of its own; HEAD, objects/ and refs/ are what makes a
        directory a git directory.  Its files are checked first only when the cheap test fails.

      sourcetree_clones <root>
        the clone directories below <root>, sorted
        A root which is itself a repository is the single clone; otherwise every direct
        sub-directory of one is a clone.  A project directory without a repository, a build or
        vendor directory and a file in between are passed over.

      sourcetree_exclude <clone>
        the sources keeping '.worktrees/' out: none|local|gitignore|local+gitignore
        The convention is a line in '.git/info/exclude': the tree is the user's business and must
        never be committed.  A '.gitignore' carrying '.worktrees/' is the tracked variant the
        machinery writes on first use -- reported, never repaired.

      sourcetree_worktrees - 'git worktree list --porcelain' on stdin, one record per tree
        One pass over the porcelain listing: '<path> <HEAD> <branch> <flags> <lock reason>' per
        tree, fields separated by US (0x1f).  A path is printed as git reports it (absolute) and
        the branch without its 'refs/heads/' prefix; the flags are 'locked', 'prunable' and 'bare'
        in that order.  'detached' is not a flag: a tree without a branch carries an empty branch
        field.  US and not a tab, because a tab is IFS whitespace and 'read' would collapse the
        empty fields this carries (see 'sourcetree_record').

      sourcetree_changes <tree>
        '<changed> <untracked>' or '? ?' when git refuses
        Counted from 'git status --porcelain': git quotes a path with an unusual name, so one
        reported entry is one line.  Untracked entries are counted apart because a tree full of
        build output reads as dirty while no tracked file was touched.

      sourcetree_relation <clone> <main> <commit>
        '<unmerged> <behind> <merge base>'
        'unmerged' counts the commits of this tree which are not reachable from the main checkout:
        the work a merge would bring in.  'behind' counts the commits of the main checkout which
        are not in this tree: how far the trunk moved on.  Both are the two halves of the merge
        base ('<main>...<commit>'), which is resolved only when neither half is empty.

      sourcetree_record - append the row in SOURCETREE_ROW to SOURCETREE_ROWS
        The single record format both renderers read, fields separated by US (0x1f):
          1 abs        2 kind       3 state      4 relation   5 flags      6 branch     7 commit
          8 changed    9 untracked 10 unmerged  11 behind    12 merge base 13 exclude  14 clone
         15 lock reason
        US and not a tab: a tab is IFS whitespace, so 'read' would collapse an empty 'flags' or
        'branch' field and shift every field after it.
        The stored row carries the sort key '<rank>:<path>' in front: 'sourcetree_clone_records'
        sorts by it, so the main checkout comes first, then the clone's own worktrees and last the
        trees registered elsewhere -- all by path, which keeps two digests of the same layout line
        for line comparable.

      sourcetree_clone_records <root> <clone>
        the records of one clone, its main checkout first

      sourcetree_rows_print - print SOURCETREE_ROWS in the order of their sort key
        The rows of one clone, sorted by '<rank>:<path>' with US as the field separator: the main
        checkout first, then the clone's own worktrees, then the trees registered elsewhere (rank
        2), each group by path.  Nothing is sorted across clones: a clone's trees stay together.

      sourcetree_collect <root>
        one record per tree of every clone below <root>

      sourcetree_counts <records>
        '<clones> <worktrees> <unmerged> <dirty>'
        'dirty' counts trees with local changes, not the changes themselves; 'unmerged' sums the
        commits no main checkout has yet.

      sourcetree_state_text <root> <records> <counts>
        the aligned digest

      sourcetree_state_json <root> <records> <counts>
        the digest as one JSON object
        One object per tree.  Every field goes through the helpers above -- the ones of this module
        and the literals of Bar.d/json_lib -- which leave their result in a variable instead of
        printing it: the object of one tree quotes a dozen of them and a subshell per field would
        dominate the runtime of the machine form.

      sourcetree_state [--json] [<root>]
        the source-tree digest, see the module header
        The body of the 'sourcetree_state' rule.  The machine form is also entered with
        'bar --json sourcetree_state' (BAR_AGENT_JSON, see Bar.d/agent_rules).  An unknown argument
        is a usage error, exit 2; a root which is not a directory cannot be asked, exit 3.

    Rules:

      sourcetree_state
        The source-tree digest: one line per main checkout and worktree found below <root> (the
        argument, or '$BAR_SOURCE_ROOT'), then '(N clones, M worktrees, U unmerged, D dirty)'.  See the
        module header for the fields, the JSON shape, the exit codes and what the helper deliberately
        does not do.

  ssh - Access remote hosts

    Functions:

      ssh_ping [[user@]host]
        Checks if one can connect to a remote host.

      ssh_cmd [ssh_args]..
        Executes a ssh command.

      ssh_bg_cmd [ssh_args]..
        Executes a ssh command in the background.

  std_lib - Library of general functions which are always present

    Functions:

      memo [-c|-d] [cmd args..]..
        memoize the result/stdout/stderr of commands, will always return the same result again
        Memoizes for the current run only. See memodb for persistent memoization.
        Memoized functions must be pure and only depend on their arguments and the current working directory.
        Can not handle null bytes in stdout/stderr, use memodb when that is required.

      memofn <functionnames..>
        rewrites a function into a function that uses 'memo'
        This renames the given functions to 'nomemo_<functionname>' and creates a wrapper
        function with the original name that memoizes the result.

      is_scalar <name>
        Checks that a variable 'name' is a scalar (non array) variable.

      called_as [pattern..]
        Checks whenever bar is called as a symlink matching the given patterns.
        This is usable as a rule dependency.

      is_command_installed <command>
        Checks whenever a function exists or a shell command is in PATH.

      bar_now Returns the current timestamp in microseconds since epoch.

      hash_args <arguments..>
        returns the sha1hash of all supplied arguments

      iset <target> [values]..
        indirect assign values to a variable given by name, assigns to an array when more values are given.
        When target is empty or '[*]' (missing name) then this is a no-op. Otherwise target
        must be a declared variable When no values are given then target is cleared.

  std_rules - Standard rule targets where other modules can hook in
    
    Here we define targets which can be universally used. Other modules will hook their more
    specific rules into these. Usually projects support only a subset of these standard targets.
    This is fine. Any not hooked rule will only print a debug message and being a no-op otherwise.
    
    Undesired std_rules can be renamed or deleted in the rulefile, this is preferred to
    editing the std_rules file because it allows for easier updates of the std_rules file. All
    these std rules just structure the targets and give debug output on the leafs. Actual
    actions are hooked in by other modules.

    Rules:

      all
        Rule that builds the software, documentation and necessary artifacts but will not do any testing.

      lints
        Run all linters.

      build
        Build libraries and executables, but not tests, docs and other non mandatory things.

      tests
        Run tests, first unit tests then integration tests.

      doc
        Build the documentation. When supported the documentation is linted as well.

      bench
        Build and run benchmarks.

      agent
        The checks an agent should run before and after it changes something.  Projects hook their
        targets in here (the default rulefile hooks 'lints tests'), the terse output contract for a
        human or an agent is available via 'bar check agent' / 'bar verdict agent'.

      fmt
        Reformat the source code in place.

      fix
        Apply trivial fixes, should be non breaking changes.

      update
        Update dependencies.

      run
        Run the main binary if any. This already depends on 'build' but the actual run action has
        to be hooked into 'run' by the user in the rulefile.

      audit
        Audit for security issues.

      lint_sources
        Run a static analyzer over the source code.

      lint_docs
        Checks the documentation.

      fetch_resources
        Whenever resources are externally managed and not part of the versioned source tree they
        should be added to this rule.

      build_assets
        Whenever assets that are not part of the normal build process have to be build, this should
        be added to this rule.

      build_libs
        Build libraries.

      build_bins
        Build executables.

      build_tests
        Build all tests

      build_unit_tests
        Build unit tests.

      build_integration_tests
        Build integration tests.

      build_docs
        Build the documentation

      build_benches
        Build benchmarks.

      build_examples
        Build examples.

      build_versioned_artifacts
        Usually versioned files should be source and not generated by any rule. Sometimes one wants
        exceptions from this rule. Like generating a README or CHANGELOG. This can be hooked into
        this rule. This can be run before committing or as part of the commit check where one checks
        for git_is_clean after running generate_versioned. When this failed then the user forgotten
        to update the versioned files.

      test_units
        Run unit tests.

      test_integrations
        Run integration tests.

      test_expensive
        Some tests are very expensive. Since building and running makes no much difference then
        this is accumulated in this single rule. This allows to exclude expensive tests from the
        normal workflow rules and include them on demand.

      testrelease
        Runs the 'release_tests' in a testdir. Uses the current index (staged or last commit) This
        is not a full release test as it wont attempt to bump version and does not generate
        changelogs etc.

      benchmark
        Running benchmarks. This does not include building benchmarks, see the 'bench' rule.

      deploy
        Deploy the project to some server or similar.

      clean
        Clean all build artifacts, but keep assets and user configuration.

      force_reset
        Danger-Zone: reset the project into a pristine state with all non versioned data deleted or
        reset to the original state.

      activate
        Activates maintainer curated githooks and other setup to use bar in a checked out
        repository.

      --help
        Show help.

      -h
        Show help.

      --debug [rule] [args]
        Evaluate a rule with at debug level.

      --trace [rule] [args]
        Evaluate a rule with at trace level.

      --verbose [rule] [args]
        Evaluate a rule at increased verbosity level.

      --quiet [rule] [args]
        Evaluate a rule with supressed output.

  testdir - Creating an isolated directory for testing
    
    Unlike other frameworks which do 'pre-commit' processing bar will not modify, block or
    stash the users workdir. Instead it creates and manages directories which become populated
    with the projects files. These testdirs also act as caches for build artifacts an can be
    seeded from previous runs to increase build/test speed. Testdirs are automatically garbage
    collected, only a few most recent used ones are kept for inspection and seeding new build.
    
    Testdirs are created from git 'treeish' objects. Thus git is mandatory.
    Testdirs are created in the git toplevel and have the name constructed from
    '$TESTDIR_PREFIX-<timestamp>-<treehash>/'

    Variables:

      TESTDIR_PREFIX (gx)
        Prefix used for testdirs.
        Default: ${TESTDIR_PREFIX:-.test}

      TESTDIR_KEEP (gx)
        How many old testdirs.
        Default: ${TESTDIR_KEEP:-5}

      TESTDIR (gx)
        Path to the current testdir.

      TESTDIR_PREV (gx)
        Path to the previous testdir if exits.

    Functions:

      testdir_gc Cleanup old testdirs.
        Removed old unused testdirs. This is called automatically. It is only necessary to
        manually call this when testdirs piled up because they where in use or when
        'TESTDIR_KEEP' was decremented.

      testdir_clean Cleanup all testdirs.
        Removes all unused testdirs.

      testdir_list Lists all testdirs.

    Rules:

      testdir_enter [treeish]
        Sets up a test directory change dir into it.
        Must be called from the git toplevel. Takes an optional treeish to construct the testdir
        from. When no treeish is given then current index is used.  After creation the
        'testdir_enter_hook' rule is evaluated. This rule is used to prep/populate/seed the
        testdir. When a previous testdir exists then 'TESTDIR_PREV' point to it, the hook can use
        that as source for populating already build artifacts.

  tty_lib - Module for terminal control codes
    
    The codes are two associative arrays with the same names: 'TTYCTL' maps a style name to the
    escape sequence that produces it, 'TTYNIL' maps every one of those names to the empty string.
    'TTYOUT' and 'TTYERR' are names for one of the two, decided once when the module loads:
    'TTYCTL' when the stream is a terminal and colour was not refused, 'TTYNIL' otherwise.  A
    caller writes '${TTYERR[fail]}text${TTYERR[reset]}' and never tests a terminal itself.
    
    The names are styles, not colours: what 'fail' looks like is this module's decision, and a
    project which wants a different look replaces the table instead of rewriting every message.
    
      reset      ends a styled run
      label      the tag of a line, 'RUN:', 'CHECK:'
      ok         a check passed, 'OK:', 'CHECK:'
      fail       a check failed or an unexpected problem, 'FAIL:', 'ERROR:'
      panic      a fatal problem, 'PANIC:'
      warn       'WARN:', 'NOTE:'
      info       'INFO:'
      debug      'DEBUG:'
      trace      'TRACE:'
      dbg        the 'DBG:' line
      strike     struck-through text inside a 'CHECK:'
      clear_eol  clears from the cursor to the end of the line
      clear_line column one, then clears the line
      cr         column one of the same line
    
    There is no fallback for a name the table does not have: the call sites write
    '${TTYERR[fail]}' without a ':-' and bar runs under 'set -u', so a misspelt style is an error
    naming it, not a message which quietly lost its colour.
    
    ADDING A STYLE
    
    'TTYCTL' and 'TTYNIL' are writable, and 'tty_style' sets a name in both at once:
    
      tty_style mine $'\e[38;5;33m'     # the escape sequence itself, not a description of it
      echo "${TTYOUT[mine]}mine${TTYOUT[reset]}"
    
    The value is the sequence, the empty string goes to 'TTYNIL', so a style a project adds prints
    nothing when the stream is not a terminal -- the same rule the built in ones follow.
    
    THE CODES
    
    A style is the CSI sequence '\e[' <parameter> 'm'.  The parameters are the SGR (Select Graphic
    Rendition) numbers below, separated by ';' when there is more than one of them:
    
      0  reset                 1  bold              2  dim                3  italic
      4  underline             5  blink             7  reverse            9  strike-through
      22 neither bold nor dim  23 italic off        24 underline off
      27 reverse off           29 strike-through off
    
      30-37 foreground            40-47 background
      90-97 bright foreground    100-107 bright background
      38;5;<n> 256 colours foreground     48;5;<n> 256 colours background
      39 default foreground        49 default background
    
    The eight colours of 30-37 and 40-47 are black, red, green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan and
    white, 90-97 and 100-107 are those eight in their bright form.  'warn' is '\e[35;1m', magenta
    and bold, and the 'std_lib' module is the example of how the names are used.

    Functions:

      tty_style <name> <sequence>
        Defines a style, or replaces one.
        <sequence> is the escape sequence itself, e.g. $'\e[38;5;33m'; the codes are in the module
        documentation above.  The name goes into 'TTYCTL' with that sequence and into 'TTYNIL' with
        the empty string, so it disappears when the stream is not a terminal.

      tty_echo [echo_args]
        Echos only when the output is a tty.

  watch_rules - Continious supervision by a file watcher

    Rules:

      watch [rule [arguments..]]
        Evaluates a rule or MAIN whenever a versioned files changed in the current directory.
        File watchers hook into this rule.

      watch_git_index [rule [arguments..]]
        Evaluates a rule or MAIN whenever a the git index changed.
        File watchers hook into this rule.


INITIAL INSTALLATION

  Bar can be invoked in different ways:

  1.  Installed in $PATH:
      To make this work it is best to clone bar locally and symlink the checked out files
      to your '.local/' tree. This allows easy upgrades via git:

      When you have radicle (https://radicle.xyz/) installed you can clone it with:

       rad clone rad:z3WhBdHt1VVNyeQ61zpY66pNzuSrP

      Otherwise it can be cloned by git with:

       git clone https://seed.pipapo.org/z3WhBdHt1VVNyeQ61zpY66pNzuSrP.git bar

      Then you can install it in your ~/.local tree with:

       cd bar
       ./bar init_install ~/.local/

      This creates symlinks to 'bar', 'please', 'Bar.d', 'Barf.default', 'Please.default' in ~/.local
      and symlinks 'Bar.d/*' to '~/.config/please/'.  Existing symlinks are left untouched and only
      the ones for modules which are not linked yet are added, so a module which was added to
      'Bar.d/' later becomes reachable by running 'init_install' again (no manual symlink needed).

      Now 'bar' is installed, check it with 'bar help'

  2.  The local './bar' initialized from above:
      Since bar is a bash script, it’s easy to version and ship it with other software.
      To initialize it in a current directory use either of:

       bar init          # creates bar, Barf, Bar.d/
       bar init --hidden # creates .bar, .Barf, .Bar.d/

      This installs the modules bar needs at startup and then completes 'Bar.d/' from the installed
      'Barf' ('bar init_deps').  With 'bar init --no-install-deps' that last step is left out, so
      the 'Barf' can be customized first: 'bar init_deps' afterwards vendors the modules the
      edited 'Barf' names.

      After that Barf can be customized, unnecessary modules in Bar.d/ may be deleted ('bar
      modules_prune' deletes the ones which are still unmodified copies of the installed ones and
      completes the tree again).  Once that is done these files should be put under version
      control.

      When the installed bar from 1. becomes updated then a local version can be updated by:

       bar init --update

      This only updates 'bar' and any existing modules in 'Bar.d/' but will not touch the
      'Barf' file since that is meant for local customization.

       bar init_update_merge_barf

      Will do a merge of the installed 'Barf' file with the local one. This *will* leave 3-way
      conflict markers behind when there are changes. These must be manually resolved!

      The updated files should then be committed to version control.

  3.  githooks symlinked from './.git/hooks/*' -> '../../bar'
      'bar' has support to be used as githooks. This needs manual enabling. Individual hooks can be
      enabled or disabled with 'bar githook_enable <hook>' and 'bar githook_disable <hook>'.
      For existing projects using 'bar' a maintainer can setup rules to activate all necessary hooks.
      This then can be activated by './bar activate'.


GIT HOOK ACTIVATION

  Bar will be inert in a project that ships with bar initialized. One can call it manually by
  './bar'.  A maintainer of the project may define 'activate' rules that activate
  githooks. These are then activated by './bar activate'. This will create symlinks in the
  '.git/hooks/' directory to the 'bar' script. These hooks will then be executed when the
  respective git hook is triggered. The rules for these hooks are defined in the 'Barf' file
  in the project directory.


RULE SEMANTICS

  Rules are a named, ordered collection of clauses. They are considered pure with inputs being
  the current directory name and the rule's arguments. Note that the content of files is *not*
  considered and the outcome of the rule should not depend on external variables. Rules are
  evaluated lazily, only once; the result of a rule is cached. This means that rules, esp. the
  actions within the body must have deterministic sematics. For performance reasons this is
  not enforced but one may observe surprising behavior when this requirement is violated.

  Rules will be autoloaded on demand from modules in 'Bar.d/' (see below AUTOMATIC MODULE
  LOADING). For rules where this fails a fallback exits that creates an implicit rule for any
  command and shell function defined with a body calling the respective command.

  Each clause in a rule has its own set of dependencies. Dependencies are other rules which
  are evaluated in order. When a (normal) dependency fails then rule it is considered to be
  failed as well and no further attempts to evaluate following dependencies and clauses are
  made.

  Dependencies can be used as 'checking' dependency which either expects the dependency to
  succeed (suffixed with a '?') or fail (prefixed with a '!'). When such a checking dependency
  fails then the rest of the current clause is skipped. This allows conditional evaluation.
  Further dependencies may be tagged as unconditional by suffix them when a tilde '~', these
  are just evaluated but rules evaluation will proceed unconditionally even if such a
  dependency fails.

  Finally every clause can have an optional body. This are shell commands executed when all
  dependencies succeeded. Because of quotiung in shell becomes a but unwieldly there are
  shortcuts to make the body refer to shell functions, this should be preferred.


RULE DEFINITION SYNTAX

   rule [[--rule-flag].. <name>:] [--clause-flag].. [[!]deps[?|~][??] args..]..
   rule [[--rule-flag].. <name>:] [--clause-flag].. [[!]deps[?|~][??] args..].. -
   rule [[--rule-flag].. <name>:] [--clause-flag].. [[!]deps[?|~][??] args..].. -- [body..]

  * [--rule-flag]

    Sets flags for a rule. Flags are additive and affect the rule, not single clauses.

    --always
      The result is not cached and the rule will be always evaluated again.

    --bare
      When this rule is called as initial rule (bar <rule>) then do not eval SETUP,
      PREPROCESS, POSTPROCESS and CLEANUP.

    --reverse
      The clauses of this rule will be evaluated in reverse order

    --disjunct
      Makes the clauses of this rule disjunctive. The rule will succeed with the first
      successful clause. Normally clauses are conjunctive and fail with the first failing
      clause.

    --meta
      Metarules do not have their own clause local variable. This allow them to modify
      the clause locals of the calling rule. 'clause_local' for example is a metarule.
      User defined rules that want to modify clause locals will need to be meta too.
      See 'try_cargo_toolchain' for an example.

  * [<name>:]

    A rule can have a optional name (suffixed with a colon).  When the name is not given it
    defaults to 'MAIN'.  Rules that have only a name but no dependencies and no body create a
    body that calls a bash command or function with the same name as the rule passing rule
    arguments to it.

  * [--clause-flag]..

    Sets flags affecting this clause only.

    --conclusive
      Makes a clause result conclusive, if not skipped no more clauses will be tried.

    --default
      Default clauses will be kept at the end of the list of clauses. Using '--default'
      together with 'disjunct' rules allows one to hook up action while maintaining a fallback
      or warning/error when no hooked up rule succeeded.

  * [[!]deps[?|~][??] args..]..

    Dependencies, are a list of other rules that this rule depends on. These will be executed
    in order. If any of the dependencies fails then this rule is considered failed as well, no
    further dependencies, bodies or clauses are executed unless this dependency is a checking
    or unconditional one.

    A dependency can be either prefixed with an exclamation mark or suffixed with question
    marks or a tilde. The exclamation mark prefix expects the dependency to fail and will
    continue then, if the dependency succeeds then the remaining dependencies and the rule
    body are skipped. With a question mark as suffix the dependency is expected to succeed,
    when it fails then the rest of the dependencies and the rule body is skipped. With a tilde
    as suffix outcome of the dependencny is ignored and the rest of the dependencies and the
    rule body is executed.  The difference here is that these checking dependencies decide if
    a clause should be skipped or succeed while a failure on a normal dependency will fail a
    rule instantly. When a rule is suffixed with two question marks then this behaves like
    "'rule_exists? dep' dep". Making this dependency optional. This can be combined with the
    another question mark, a tilde, or explamation mark prefix. This is commonly used to
    define optional rule hooks.

    Dependencies can have arguments. When provided then the dependency and its arguments must
    be quoted. These arguments are passed in the 'RULE_ARGS' array to the body of the rule.

  * -

    When there is a single hypen '-' at the end of a rule definition then the rule body is a
    call to a command or function of the same name as the rule with all arguments passed. This
    is used when one wants to add dependencies to a existing command or function.

  * -- [body..]

    After a double hyphen a optional rule body follows. This are the commands to
    execute for this clause. If these fail then the rule is considered failed. When a name but
    no body and no dependencies are given then the body defaults to the rule name. This makes
    it easy to translate simple parameterless commands and functions to rules. Usually the
    body has to be quoted to prevent shell expansions at definition time. Later when a rule
    becomes evaluated the body is passed to 'eval'.

    When the body is omitted, then the rule body is read from stdin. This allows to use
    heredocs or herestrings or read the body from a file.

    When a rule overrides an existing command a 'NOTE' will be printed on higher verbosity
    levels. The user is responsible for sensible overiding of commands.

  Rules must not have mutually recursive dependencies. When such is detected the
  evaluation aborts.

  Examples:

   # Add a clause to 'MAIN'
   rule -- echo hello

   # Different ways to define rule bodies
   rule foo_ok: -- echo inline
   rule foo_ok: -- '
       echo inline quoted
   '
   rule foo_ok: -- <<<"echo herestring"
   rule foo_ok: -- <<EOR
      echo heredoc
   EOR

   # Make a rule that fails
   rule foo_fail: -- false

   # functions and commands can be used as rules, this should be preferred
   function example
   {
       echo "i am example called with $*"
   }

   # add a tests rule with 3 clauses
   rule tests: foo_ok? 'example "argument"' -- echo "foo_ok success"
   rule tests: !foo_ok -- echo "This is never called, but MAIN still passes"
   rule tests: foo_fail? -- echo "This is also never called"

   # add tests to MAIN
   rule tests

  For some more examples see the 'example' file that ships with bar.


  Special Rules and Names

    Rules that do not have a name fall back to 'MAIN', when no rule name is given at execution
    time then 'MAIN' will be evaluated.

    If exist 'SETUP', 'PREPROCESS', 'POSTPROCESS' and 'CLEANUP' will be called.
    * SETUP
      Should set up the environment for all subsequent evaluation. Rules in 'SETUP'
      should be self-sufficient and not expect a working environment yet.
      A failure here will abort bar.
    * PREPROCESS
      Doing any preparatory tasks within the set up environment.
      A failure here will abort bar.
    * MAIN
      When the bar is not called via a symlink or the user did not provide a rule to
      evaluate, then MAIN is the default rule. Otherwise the user supplied or symlink-deduced
      rulename will be evaluated. The result of this rule determines the exitcode of bar.
    * POSTPROCESS
      Is for evaluating things after the main rule finished. The environment and
      state is still the same as in the main rule. A failure here will be reported but not
      reflected in the exitode.
    * CLEANUP
      Is for removing temporary resources and doing any other kind of cleanup work. This
      means the state and environment at cleanup time may be differen/partially destructed and
      should not be relied upon. Clauses in the CLEANUP rule are evaluated in reverse order of
      their definition. A failure here will be reported but not reflected in the exitcode.

    After a rule that is not flagged with '--always' got evaluated it is not allowed to add
    more clauses to it anymore, as the outcome would be unexpected and impure.


  Keep-Going Mode

    Bar supports a keep-going facility via the BAR_KEEP_GOING environment variable. When
    enabled, clauses that fail do not immediately halt rule evaluation—subsequent clauses
    continue to execute, though the overall failure is still propagated.

    BAR_KEEP_GOING can be set to a number which is decremented on each failure, when this
    reachs zero no more failures are accepted and the evaluation will terminate.

    When BAR_KEEP_GOING set to anything else than a number then evaluation will accept
    infinite failures.

    When not set then it defaults to one then evaluation will terminate on the
    first failure.

    Clauses marked '--conclusive' immediately halt keep-going.


  Purity Requirements

    All rules and functions must be sequentiually pure in their success branch. This means
    side effects must not alter the behavior of subsequent rules when they fail. For rules
    where this may be the case, like rules changing directories they must rather die than
    proceed with a undefined state in case of a failure.


RULE ENVIRONMENT

  Rules implicitly depend on the arguments passed and the current directory they run in. Any
  other state or environment variable is not considered. When the result of a rule would
  depend on such external state then the rule is impure and may lead to wrong
  results.


MODULES

  Modules are shell snippets normally located in '$BAR_DIR' ('Bar.d/', 'bar.d/', '.Bar.d/' or
  '.bar.d/') when called as 'bar' or symlink or '~/.config/please' when called as 'please'.
  The 'std_lib' and 'rules_lib' are always loaded at startup. Modules matching '*_rules' are
  also autoloaded at startup. Any other '*_lib' must be manuallly loaded with 'require'.
  Modules with simple alphabetic names without underscores are lazy loaded on demand.

  Modules are loaded with the 'require' function. This ensures that they are loaded only once.
  Require will load modules outside of '$BAR_DIR' when a path containing at least one slash
  is given.

  The first variant for modules that are initially loaded is used for modules that define
  helper functions that don't have associated rules or rules that are primarly used for
  checking conditions before delegating to the actual rules which do the work.

  The second variant is for mudules that define rules which are self-contained and can't
  extend existing rules with new clauses. Here are the rules and functions which do the actual
  work.

  The reason for this is that some will add clauses to existing rule which must be done before
  the rules are evaluated while we can improve performance by lazy loading modules that are
  not always needed.

  The module loader derives the module name from the rule name by removing any prefixes and
  suffixes. Thus the rules which shall trigger loading must follow the naming schema with the
  module name after the prefixes in the rule name.

  Per convention '<name>_rules' is a module that contains the rules (and few functions) that
  that add clauses to other rules to make 'name' accessible. These '*_rules' files are
  autoloaded at startup.

  '<name>_lib' are libraries for support functions other modules may use (with 'require
  name_lib').


AUTOMATIC MODULE LOADING

  Rule names may include underscores, then the word before the first underscore is used to
  determine a module name used for auto-loading rules. They can have special prefixes which
  are removed when auto-loading:

  - 'is_', 'has_' and 'try_' are reserved for check rules they don't have any special
    semantic except for being stripped at auto loading.

  When a rule is not found then a module name is derived from the rule name by removing the
  'try_', 'is_', 'has_' prefixes and cutting off anything behind the first
  underscore. Eg. 'try_module_check' results in 'module'. This is then searched as
  'Bar.d/module', 'bar.d/module', '.Bar.d/module' or '.bar.d/module' (or respective please
  variants) and loaded if present. Thus modules that are automatically loaded must be named by
  single word without underscores.


STANDARD RULES

  Bar ships with a module that defines a set of standard rules where other modules can add
  clauses to. Check 'Bar.d/std_rules' for details.

  'SETUP', 'PREPROCESS', 'MAIN', 'POSTPROCESS' and 'CLEANUP' are special rule names called
  approbiately.

  The 'clause_local [var[=value]]' is for defining variables that are local to a clause.  Only
  scalars can be clause local. Ideally this should be used as dependency and setting a a
  variable to a value. It can be used in a function as well esp when creating other metarules.


HELPER REPERTOIRE

  Many recurring questions -- a filter, a retrieval, a listing, a count, a state snapshot -- have
  one deterministic answer which would otherwise be re-derived with several commands and a lot of
  context.  Such an answer is a 'helper': a rule of a 'helper module' which names itself with a
  '# helper-module:' header in its first lines and registers each of its rules with a
  '# helper:', '# helper-bar:' or '# helper-please:' declaration above it -- the three forms
  share their four fields and differ only in which personality ('bar' or 'please') lists the
  helper, and every listed row is prefixed with that personality.

   bar helpers          # the repertoire of this installation, one line per helper plus its count
   bar helpers --json   # the same repertoire as one JSON object, for a wrapper
   bar helpers --check  # verify that every helper module is registered (also run by 'lints')

  The repertoire is generated by reading the module sources: listing it loads no module and runs
  no helper, and 'please helpers' works from any directory -- it even appends the 'bar'
  repertoire of the directory it is run in (a 'bar helpers' there), again without dedup.  See
  'Bar.d/helpers_rules' for the naming rules (a helper rule loads its module by its own first
  word), the registration format, the output conventions and the elision and exit code
  requirements every helper has to follow.


MAIN API

  bar - The main BAsh Rulez script
    
    Provides only basic functionality for module loading and debugging and starting rule
    evaluation. Loads the 'std_lib' and 'rule_lib' to make them omnipresent.

    Functions:

      bar_main [rulefile] [--bare] [rule [arguments..]]
        Loads a rulefile and executes rule with optional arguments
        [rulefile]           - File to load the user rules from.
                               Default: Barf|barf|.Barf|.barf when called as bar or symlink.
                                        Pleasef|pleasef|.Pleasef|.pleasef|\$HOME/.Pleasef when called as please.
        [--bare]             - Don't eval SETUP, PREPROCESS, POSTPROCESS and CLEANUP.
        [rule [arguments..]] - Target rule with arguments.
                               Default: MAIN

      bar_origin_dir - the 'Bar.d' of the installation bar was run from, when it is not the one in use
        A project may carry its own (vendored) 'Bar.d', then the installation bar was run from is
        where modules missing from the project are taken from.  When bar runs from its
        installation there is no origin.  'please' has no vendored copy either: its modules live
        in a single, absolute directory.

      bar_vendor_modules <modpath>
        installs a module of Bar.d which is missing from the origin, prints what it installed
        <modpath> - The path the module would have in 'Bar.d' (a glob is expanded there).
        Copies the module(s) from the origin installation into 'Bar.d'.  An existing module is
        never touched (that is what '--update' of the init rules is for) and the origin is never
        written to, installing only ever adds a copy here.  Prints the installed paths, returns
        non-zero when there is no origin, the name is not a module of this 'Bar.d' or the origin
        does not have it either.

      require [--opt] [modules..]
        Loads modules.
        [--opt]     - Make the modules optional, when they do not exist then no warning is emitted
                      and no failure returned.
        [modules..] - List of module names to be loaded. Modules are loaded only once, further
                      attempts to load it will be a no-op.  A name may be a glob which expands
                      to several modules.
        A module which is missing from the 'Bar.d' in use is taken from the installation bar was
        run from (see :func:`bar_vendor_modules`) and loaded from there.  When there is no origin
        or the module does not exist there either it is missing: with '--opt' that is silent and
        succeeds, without it a warning is emitted and a failure returned.

      bar_report_condense - True when the source location should be condensed on the console.
        The verbose 'file:line:func:' location is only condensed when the output goes to the
        console. Redirected output keeps the full, easily filterable format.

      bar_report_loc [N]
        Sets BAR_REPORT_LOC for a report N frames up the stack.
        Like 'source_info N' but condenses the source location of consecutive reports from the
        same file and function: the location is printed once and then replaced by blanks of the
        same width. Sets 'BAR_REPORT_REPEATED' when this report continued such a block.

      bar_report_condensed - True when the repeated location is to be blanked down to a ':'.
        'BAR_REPORT_STYLE=tag' (default) keeps the message tag in front of the blanked location,
        'colon' blanks the tag as well so that only the ':' of the location remains.

      DBG [message..]
        For print-style debugging, should not be present in production code.

      die [message..]
        Prints 'message' to stderr and exits with failure
        Unexpected fatal problem. Will terminate execution, CLEANUP rules will be called.
        The source location is condensed like 'error' does, see there for 'BAR_REPORT_CONDENSE' and
        'BAR_REPORT_STYLE'.

      error [message..]
        Print a error message to stderr.
        Unexpected but not fatal.
        The source location is printed for the first report of a function and condensed to blanks
        for the following ones, so a multi line report stays readable. 'BAR_REPORT_CONDENSE' is
        'always', 'never' or 'auto' (default: only on a console, redirected output keeps the full
        format) and 'BAR_REPORT_STYLE' is 'tag' (default) or 'colon'.

      warn [message..]
        Print an warning to stderr.
        Expected, non fatal problem.

      note [message..]
        Print an important notice to stderr.
        Important message the user should be notified about.

      info [message..]
        Print an informal message to stderr.
        Broader message about non rule related progress.

      debug [message..]
        Print a debug message to stderr.
        Finer grained progress messages.

      trace [message]
        Prints a trace message to stderr.
        Very verbose function call or finer messages.


DOCUMENTATION WRITING GUIDELINES

  Bar uses a structured documentation format that serves both human readers and automated
  tools like help generation and bash completion. Documentation uses '##' comment markers.

  File-Level Documentation:

    Use '###' at the start of a file to describe the module/file:

     ### This module provides cargo/rust support.

  Variable Documentation:

    Variables declared with 'declare' can be documented on the same line:

     declare -g MYVAR="value" ## Description of the variable

  Function Documentation:

    Functions should have documentation in the form:

     function name ## [--opt] <required> [optional..] - Short description

    The parameter list uses a formal syntax:
    - <param>    - Mandatory parameter (prototype)
    - [param]    - Optional parameter (prototype)
    - <param..>  - One or more occurrences (suffix .. after closing >)
    - <param>..  - One or more occurrences (suffix .. after closing >)
    - [param..]  - Zero or more occurrences (suffix .. after closing ])
    - [param]..  - Zero or more occurrences (suffix .. after closing ])
    - <> and []  - Can be nested
    - word       - Anything not in <> or [] is a literal (like 'as', 'into', ':')
    - --flag     - Literal flag (can be in <--flag> for prototype completion)
    - -f         - Literal short flag
    - a|b        - Alternatives where a and b can be any of the above
                   (a|b|c|d etc. any number allowed)
                   Note: <a..|b> is at least one 'a' or a single 'b'
                         <a|b>.. is at least one of 'a' or 'b'
    - param      - Parameter names serve as prototypes for completion

    Examples:

    - function rename ## <this> as|into <that> - rename this as/into that
      Requires literal 'as' or 'into' at the 2nd position.
    - function process ## <input:> <output> - process input to output
      The colon ':' after <input> is a literal character.

    Parameter identifiers start with an alphabetic character followed by [[:alnum:]-_].
    These serve as prototypes for completion and are refined in following documentation.

    Multi-line documentation continues on following lines starting with '##':

     function foo ## [--verbose|-v] <input> [output] - Process files
     {
         ## [--verbose|-v] - Enable verbose output
         ## <input>         - Input file to process
         ## [output]        - Optional output file (defaults to stdout)
         ...
     }

  Rule Documentation:

    Rules are documented with '##' lines before the rule definition:

     ## <target> - Build the specified target
     rule build:

    For rules with parameters, use the same formal syntax as functions:

     ## [--toolchain] <target> [options..] - Build target
     rule build:

  Documentation Prototypes for Completion:

    Parameter identifiers map to completion functions. Common prototypes include:

    - <file>      - Any file on filesystem
    - <directory> - Any directory
    - <path>      - Any valid path
    - <text>      - Any text input
    - <number>    - Numeric input
    - <rule>      - Existing rule name
    - <command>   - Command or function name

    Module-specific prototypes can be defined (e.g., <toolchain> in cargo module).
    See contrib/bar_complete for completion implementation details.
    
  Defining Custom Completion Prototypes:

    Modules can define custom prototypes for parameter completion using single-hash comments
    that must start at column 0 (no leading whitespace):
    
      # prototype: "mytype" = "file"
      # prototype: "myfile" = "file existing local"
      # prototype: "mycommand" = "command"
      # prototype: "toolchain" = "extcomp cargo"
      # prototype: "gitargs" = "extcomp git"
    
    These definitions register a prototype with a completer. The format is:

      # prototype: "prototype_name" = "completer_spec"
    
    Where completer_spec is either:
    - A built-in completer name (file, directory, path, rule, command, etc.)
    - A completer with predicates (e.g., "file existing local")
    - "ext function_name" for external/custom completers (calls bar --bare function_name)
    - "extcomp command" for black-box external command completion (invokes command's native bash completion)
    
    The "extcomp" type enables leveraging any command's existing bash completion by invoking
    it as a black box. For example, "extcomp git" will use git's native completion for all
    git subcommands and flags. This works with git, cargo, ssh, and any other command that
    has bash completion support.
    
    Note: The '# prototype:' comment must start at the beginning of the line (column 0).
    
    These are registered as "module@prototype" in the completion registry, allowing
    module-specific completion behavior while maintaining fallback to global prototypes.


BASH COMPLETION

  Bar supports bash completion for rulefiles, rules, functions, and their arguments. Completion
  is automatically installed when you run 'bar init_install'.

  The completion script is installed to '~/.bash_completion/bar_complete'. To enable it,
  either source it in your .bashrc:

   source ~/.bash_completion/bar_complete

  Or if you have bash-completion package installed, it should be automatically loaded.

  Completion works for:

  - bar <TAB>       - completes rulefiles, rules, and functions in current directory
  - please <TAB>    - completes rulefiles, rules, and functions
  - ./bar <TAB>     - completes rulefiles, rules, and functions from local bar

  The completion logic will:

  1.  Find any user rule files in current directory (files containing 'function' or 'rule',
      or with shebang pointing to bar/please)
  2.  Complete available bash functions and rules from default rule file and Bar.d/ modules
  3.  Provide context-aware argument completion based on parameter types


